QB.add(36,[{"id":36001,"t":"What Is Immunity","s":"A patient with metastatic melanoma is started on a treatment that boosts the body's own defences against the tumour. Which part of the definition of immunity does this rely on?","o":["Defence against catastrophic cell dysfunction such as cancer","Defence against foreign cells and proteins introduced into the body from outside","Defence against infectious agents such as viruses","Tolerance towards harmless environmental antigens","Defence against commensal organisms of the skin"],"e":"Immunity is the ability of an organism to defend itself against three things: infectious agents, foreign cells and proteins, and catastrophic cell dysfunction such as cancer. A tumour arises from the body's own cells, so attacking it relies on the third element. Immunity therefore protects against internal disease-causing problems as well as external pathogens.","w":["Foreign proteins come from outside the body, whereas melanoma cells arise from the patient's own cells.","Melanoma is not an infectious agent such as a bacterium, virus or parasite.","Tolerance is non-reactivity, which is the opposite of mounting an attack on the tumour.","Commensal organisms are tolerated, not attacked, and are unrelated to a tumour."],"k":"Immunity covers infectious agents, foreign cells/proteins and catastrophic cell dysfunction such as cancer.","rn":[["","What are the 3 things immunity defends against?","What are the 3 things immunity defends against?","Infectious agents; Foreign cells and proteins; Catastrophic cell dysfunction"],["","Catastrophic cell dysfunction: example?","Catastrophic cell dysfunction: example?","Cancer"]]},{"id":36002,"t":"What Is Immunity","s":"A 4-month-old boy has had repeated severe infections since birth, and a genetic disorder of immune function is confirmed. His cousin, an adult, has an immunodeficiency caused by a virus that attacks immune cells. Which pairing correctly classifies the two conditions?","o":["Infant: inherited (e.g. SCID); cousin: acquired (e.g. AIDS)","Infant: acquired (e.g. AIDS); cousin: inherited (e.g. SCID)","Both are inherited immunodeficiencies of the SCID type","Both are hypersensitivity states rather than immunodeficiencies","Infant: autoimmunity; cousin: immunodeficiency"],"e":"Immunodeficiency is the outcome of failed immunity. It can be inherited, as in severe combined immune deficiency (SCID), which has a genetic cause and is identified at an early age, or acquired during life, as in acquired immune deficiency syndrome (AIDS) caused by HIV. A genetic disorder presenting in infancy fits SCID, while a virus-caused deficiency in an adult fits AIDS.","w":["This reverses the classification: the infant's disorder is genetic and the adult's is virus-acquired.","The cousin's condition was acquired from a virus, so it cannot be inherited.","Hypersensitivity is an excessive response, whereas both patients have failed immunity.","Autoimmunity is an attack on self, not a failure to defend against infection."],"k":"SCID = inherited immunodeficiency in early life; AIDS = acquired immunodeficiency caused by HIV.","rn":[["","Immunodeficiency acquired during life: example?","Immunodeficiency acquired during life: example?","AIDS"],["","What does AIDS stand for?","What does AIDS stand for?","Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome"]]},{"id":36003,"t":"What Is Immunity","s":"Which of the following is NOT an example of an overactive immune system?","o":["Death from opportunistic infections in a patient with AIDS","Bronchial contraction after exposure to an allergen","Rejection of a transplanted kidney by a recipient with full immune function","B and T cells attacking self tissues after T cell control fails","Anaphylactic shock following mast cell activation"],"e":"The speaker notes list three overactive states: allergies (mast cells activated by allergens release histamines, causing redness, swelling, mucus secretion, bronchial contraction and, at worst, anaphylactic shock), graft rejection, and autoimmunity (when T cell control mechanisms fail). An underactive immune system causes immunodeficiency, in which patients die of opportunistic infections, as in AIDS.","w":["Bronchial contraction is part of the allergic response, an overactive state.","Graft rejection by a fully functional immune system is an overactive state.","Autoimmunity, where B and T cells attack self, is an overactive state.","Anaphylactic shock is the extreme form of allergy, an overactive state."],"k":"Overactive = allergy, graft rejection, autoimmunity; underactive = immunodeficiency.","rn":[["","What do patients with an underactive immune system die from?","What do patients with an underactive immune system die from?","Opportunistic infections"]]},{"id":36004,"t":"What Is Immunity","s":"A student eats peanuts and within minutes develops facial swelling, redness and wheeze. Which mechanism best explains these signs?","o":["Allergens activate mast cells, which release histamines","Complement forms membrane attack complexes on her own cells","B and T cells attack self tissues after failure of T cell control","Neutrophils release lysozyme into her airways","Her immune system fails to respond to the peanut antigen"],"e":"Allergy is an overactive response: activation of mast cells by allergens (antigens) leads to production of histamines, causing redness, swelling, mucus secretion and bronchial contraction (asthma). In extreme cases the sufferer may die of anaphylactic shock. Allergy and hypersensitivity are inappropriate responses to antigens, not failures to respond.","w":["Complement attacks invading bacteria; the speaker notes link allergy to mast cells and histamine.","Attack on self tissues after T cell control fails describes autoimmunity, not a food allergy.","Lysozyme is a barrier enzyme in tears, nasal secretions and saliva, not the cause of swelling and wheeze.","Allergy is an excessive response, not a failure to respond."],"k":"Allergy: allergen → mast cell activation → histamine → redness, swelling, mucus, bronchoconstriction.","rn":[["","What happens in an allergy?","What happens in an allergy?","Allergens activate mast cells; Histamine produced"]]},{"id":36005,"t":"Immune Tolerance","s":"A patient's immune system begins attacking the cells of their own thyroid gland. Which failure best explains this?","o":["Breakdown of tolerance to self antigens","Loss of tolerance to commensal gut flora","Inherited failure of immunity, as in SCID","Excessive tolerance towards infectious agents","Failure of physical barriers such as skin"],"e":"Tolerance is a state of immunological non-reactivity to an antigen. It protects our own cells and tissues (self antigens), harmless environmental antigens such as food and pollen, and commensal organisms. An immune response to self tissue is a breakdown of tolerance, which results in autoimmune disease.","w":["Commensal organisms are microbes such as gut flora, not the patient's own thyroid cells.","SCID is an inherited immunodeficiency, a failure to defend, not an attack on self.","Excessive tolerance to pathogens would cause failure to clear infection, not an attack on the thyroid.","Barrier failure lets pathogens in; it does not make immune cells attack self tissue."],"k":"Autoimmune disease = breakdown of tolerance to self antigens.","rn":[["","What results from an immune response to self tissue?","What results from an immune response to self tissue?","Breakdown of tolerance; Autoimmune disease"]]},{"id":36006,"t":"What Is Immunity","s":"Which of the following is NOT one of the groups of potential antigens that the immune system normally tolerates?","o":["Pathogenic bacteria invading host tissues","The body's own cells and tissues","Harmless food antigens","Pollen in the environment","Commensal skin and gut flora"],"e":"Many potential antigens are not harmful: self antigens, harmless environmental antigens such as food sources and pollen, and commensal organisms such as skin and gut flora. Immune cells are trained not to respond to these. Pathogenic (damage- or disease-causing) organisms invading host tissues are exactly what the immune system must attack.","w":["Self cells and tissues are protected by tolerance.","Food sources are listed as harmless environmental antigens.","Pollen is listed as a harmless environmental antigen.","Commensal skin and gut flora are tolerated organisms."],"k":"Tolerated antigens: self, harmless environmental (food, pollen) and commensal flora.","rn":[["","What is Infection?","What is Infection?","Invasion of host tissues by foreign organisms; May or may not cause disease"]]},{"id":36007,"t":"What Is Immunity","s":"A swab shows a microorganism living on a patient's skin without causing any disease. According to the definition used in the lecture, which statement is correct?","o":["Infection is invasion of host tissues, which may or may not cause disease","Infection by definition always produces disease in the host it invades","Only multicellular parasites can cause infection","Organisms causing infection must be viruses","Any organism present on skin is pathogenic"],"e":"The speaker notes define infection as invasion of host tissues by foreign organisms which may or may not cause disease. The body faces a variety of pathogenic (damage- or disease-causing) organisms, mostly microorganisms, so defence mechanisms are essential. Commensal organisms such as skin flora are normally tolerated and do not cause disease.","w":["The definition states infection may or may not cause disease.","Bacteria, viruses, fungi, protozoa and multicellular parasites can all cause infection.","Bacteria, fungi, protozoa and parasites also cause infection, not only viruses.","Skin commensals are tolerated and are not, by definition, pathogenic."],"k":"Infection = invasion of host tissues by foreign organisms that may or may not cause disease.","rn":[["","What is Infection?","What is Infection?","Invasion of host tissues by foreign organisms; May or may not cause disease"]]},{"id":36008,"t":"What Is Immunity","s":"Which pairing of pathogen group and example disease is correct?","o":["Protozoa: malaria","Fungi: tuberculosis","Viruses: athlete's foot","Bacteria: influenza","Multicellular parasites: thrush"],"e":"The speaker notes give examples for each group: bacteria (tuberculosis, plague), viruses (common cold, influenza), fungi (thrush, athlete's foot), protozoa (sleeping sickness, malaria) and multicellular parasites (tapeworm, flea). Malaria is the protozoan example.","w":["Tuberculosis is a bacterial example.","Athlete's foot is a fungal example.","Influenza is a viral example.","Thrush is a fungal example; tapeworm and flea are the parasites."],"k":"Bacteria TB/plague; viruses cold/flu; fungi thrush/athlete's foot; protozoa sleeping sickness/malaria; parasites tapeworm/flea.","rn":[["","What are the 5 types of organism that cause infection in humans?","What are the 5 types of organism that cause infection in humans?","Bacteria; Viruses; Fungi; Protozoa; Multicellular parasites"],["","Protozoal infections: 2 examples?","Protozoal infections: 2 examples?","Sleeping sickness; Malaria"]]},{"id":36009,"t":"Innate and Adaptive Response","s":"A child meets a new respiratory virus. The innate response begins within hours but fails to clear it, and the child becomes unwell before recovering a week later. Which explains why the innate response failed?","o":["Innate immunity cannot adapt and treats every pathogen as if new","Innate immunity takes several days to develop before it can start acting","Innate immunity relies on memory from earlier exposures","Innate immunity recognises only self antigens","Innate immunity is made up only of B and T lymphocytes"],"e":"Innate immunity responds rapidly, within hours, as the first line of defence, but its lack of ability to adapt can result in failure: it sees every pathogen as new, so sometimes it succeeds and sometimes it fails. The adaptive response then takes time to develop, eliminates the agent, allows recovery and leaves memory.","w":["Innate immunity is fast (hours); it is the adaptive response that takes time.","Innate immunity has no memory; memory belongs to adaptive immunity.","Innate cells recognise pathogen patterns, not self antigens.","B and T lymphocytes are the cells of adaptive immunity."],"k":"Innate = rapid but cannot adapt, so it can fail; adaptive = slower but broad with memory.","rn":[["","Why does innate immunity sometimes fail?","Why does innate immunity sometimes fail?","It cannot adapt; Sees every pathogen as new"]]},{"id":36010,"t":"Innate and Adaptive Response","s":"Two years after recovering from an infection, a patient is re-exposed to the same pathogen and has no illness at all. Which feature of the immune system best explains this?","o":["Adaptive memory that starts a faster response on re-encounter","Faster neutrophil production because innate cells learn from each infection they meet","Tolerance that prevents the pathogen binding host cells","Stronger physical barriers after the first infection","Lysozyme levels that rise permanently after infection"],"e":"Adaptive immunity has a memory function that kicks in quickly if a pathogen is met again later in life. On re-infection the adaptive response starts earlier than the first time, eliminating the pathogen quickly and preventing disease. Innate immunity has no memory: it treats every pathogen as new.","w":["Innate cells are not trained to respond faster on a second meeting.","Tolerance is non-reactivity, which would not protect against a pathogen.","The lecture does not describe barriers strengthening after infection.","Lysozyme is a barrier enzyme with no memory function."],"k":"Memory is a feature of adaptive immunity only.","rn":[["","What memory function does adaptive immunity show?","What memory function does adaptive immunity show?","Kicks in quickly on re-encounter; Quick elimination, disease prevented"],["","Which immune response has a memory function?","Which immune response has a memory function?","Adaptive immunity"]]},{"id":36011,"t":"Innate and Adaptive Response","a":["adaptive","adaptive immunity","adaptive immune response","acquired","acquired immunity"],"s":"Name the arm of the immune system that takes time to develop, recognises a vast variety of antigens and shows memory.","e":"Adaptive immunity is slower to develop than innate immunity but is more versatile, recognising many different antigens. It also has a memory function that kicks in quickly on re-encounter, so a second infection with the same pathogen is cleared before disease develops.","k":"Adaptive immunity: slow first time, broad, with memory.","rn":[["","Which immune response has a memory function?","Which immune response has a memory function?","Adaptive immunity"],["","Innate vs adaptive immunity: speed?","Innate vs adaptive immunity: speed?","Innate: rapid, within hours; Adaptive: takes time to develop"],["","What memory function does adaptive immunity show?","What memory function does adaptive immunity show?","Kicks in quickly on re-encounter; Quick elimination, disease prevented"]]},{"id":36012,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"A surgeon has a small cut on the hand. Why does this make infection through the skin more likely?","o":["Intact skin is impermeable to most pathogens, so a break gives access","The cut removes the lysozyme layer that the skin normally makes on its surface","Skin flora become pathogenic once the skin is cut","The cut lowers stomach pH elsewhere in the body","Mucus and cilia normally line the outer surface of skin"],"e":"Skin is impermeable to most infectious pathogens, so it is a physical barrier that stops entry. For a pathogen to infect through the skin it would generally need skin damage. Barriers are the first level of the innate response, stopping pathogens entering the body and infecting cells.","w":["Lysozyme is found in tears, nasal secretions and saliva, not described as a skin product.","Commensal flora antagonise external pathogens; the lecture does not say they become pathogenic.","Stomach pH is a separate barrier in the gut and is not affected by a hand cut.","Mucus and cilia line the respiratory tract, not the skin."],"k":"Intact skin is impermeable to most pathogens; damage provides entry.","rn":[["","Why is skin a barrier?","Why is skin a barrier?","Impermeable to most infectious pathogens"]]},{"id":36013,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"Which barrier works by breaking glycosidic bonds in the bacterial cell wall?","o":["Lysozyme in tears, nasal secretions and saliva","Low pH together with digestive enzymes in the stomach lumen","Mucus and cilia of the respiratory epithelium","Commensal organisms of the gut and vagina","Spermine present in semen"],"e":"Lysozyme is a hydrolytic enzyme present in tears, nasal secretions and saliva. It breaks down glycosidic bonds and assists in the destruction of the bacterial cell wall. The other barriers act by acid and enzymes (stomach), trapping and expulsion (mucus and cilia) or antagonism of incoming pathogens (commensal flora).","w":["Stomach acid and digestive enzymes make the environment uninhabitable and break down proteins.","Mucus traps pathogens and cilia expel them mechanically.","Commensal flora antagonise infection by external pathogens.","Spermine is a biochemical barrier in semen, but glycosidic bond breakage is the action of lysozyme."],"k":"Lysozyme breaks glycosidic bonds of the bacterial cell wall.","rn":[["","Where is lysozyme found?","Where is lysozyme found?","Tears; Nasal secretions; Saliva"]]},{"id":36014,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"A smoker has damaged ciliated epithelium in the airways and gets repeated chest infections. Which barrier is most directly impaired?","o":["Mucus trapping of pathogens with expulsion by cilia","Lysozyme-mediated breakdown of the bacterial cell wall in nasal secretions","Impermeability of the skin surface","Antagonism by commensal flora of the gut","Low pH of the gastric environment"],"e":"In the respiratory system, mucus traps pathogens, which are then expelled by the mechanical activity of the cilia on respiratory epithelial cells. Damage to the cilia stops this clearance, so trapped pathogens stay in the airways and can infect. This is a physical barrier of innate immunity.","w":["Lysozyme acts in tears, nasal secretions and saliva and is not the cilia-dependent mechanism.","Skin impermeability is not affected by airway damage.","Gut flora protect the intestine, not the airways.","Stomach acid protects the gut, not the airways."],"k":"Respiratory barrier: mucus traps pathogens, cilia expel them.","rn":[["","How does mucus protect the respiratory tract?","How does mucus protect the respiratory tract?","Traps pathogens; Expelled by epithelial cilia"]]},{"id":36015,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"After a long course of broad-spectrum treatment that clears much of a woman's normal vaginal flora, she develops an infection by an external organism. Which barrier function has been lost?","o":["Commensal flora antagonising infection by external pathogens","Lysozyme destroying the bacterial cell wall","Mucus trapping with ciliary expulsion","Impermeability of intact skin","Low stomach pH and digestive enzymes"],"e":"Intestinal flora in the gut and vagina antagonise infection from external pathogens, so loss of commensal organisms removes this biochemical barrier. The lecture lists commensal organisms in gut and vagina among the biochemical barriers, alongside lysozyme, sebaceous secretions and spermine.","w":["Lysozyme is in tears, nasal secretions and saliva, not the vaginal barrier described.","Mucus and cilia are the respiratory barrier.","The skin barrier is unaffected here.","Stomach pH protects the gut, not the vagina."],"k":"Commensal flora in gut and vagina antagonise external pathogens.","rn":[["","Where does commensal flora antagonise infection?","Where does commensal flora antagonise infection?","Gut; Vagina"]]},{"id":36016,"t":"Barrier Systems","a":["lysozyme"],"s":"Name the hydrolytic enzyme found in tears and saliva that destroys the bacterial cell wall.","e":"Lysozyme is a hydrolytic enzyme in saliva, tears and nasal secretions. It breaks glycosidic bonds and helps destroy the bacterial cell wall. It is both a biochemical barrier and one of the soluble factors of innate immunity.","k":"Lysozyme: tears, saliva, nasal secretions; breaks the bacterial cell wall.","rn":[["","Where is lysozyme found?","Where is lysozyme found?","Tears; Nasal secretions; Saliva"]]},{"id":36017,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"Which statement about the barriers of innate immunity is correct?","o":["They are the first level of the innate response and may be biochemical or physical","They are part of adaptive immunity and depend on memory","They act only after a pathogen has infected host cells","They consist mainly of B and T lymphocytes","They work only in the respiratory tract"],"e":"Barriers are the first level of the innate response: they stop pathogens entering the body and infecting cells. They can be biochemical (lysozyme, sebaceous secretions, commensal organisms, spermine) or chemical and physical (mucus, cilia, stomach acid, skin). They act at many sites, not only the airway.","w":["Barriers are innate and have no memory.","Barriers act to stop entry, before cells are infected.","B and T lymphocytes are adaptive cells, not barriers.","Barriers also include skin, stomach acid, tears and genital flora."],"k":"Barriers = first level of innate immunity, biochemical or physical, at many body sites.","rn":[["","What 2 kinds of barrier are part of innate immunity?","What 2 kinds of barrier are part of innate immunity?","Biochemical; Physical"]]},{"id":36018,"t":"Immune System Tissues","s":"A patient's red bone marrow is replaced by a tumour. Which consequence follows most directly?","o":["Reduced production of all blood cells, including lymphocytes","Loss of the main site of T-lymphocyte maturation, with other blood cells unaffected","Loss of the emergency blood store","Absence of high endothelial venules in lymph nodes","Failure of mucus and cilia barriers"],"e":"Red bone marrow is the site of production of all blood cells, including B and T lymphocytes. All blood cells start from the haematopoietic stem cell there, so bone marrow function is really important for the health of the immune system. The thymus matures T cells and the spleen is the emergency blood store.","w":["T-lymphocyte maturation is the role of the thymus.","The spleen acts as the emergency blood store.","HEVs are found in lymph nodes, not bone marrow.","Mucus and cilia are respiratory barriers unrelated to bone marrow."],"k":"Red bone marrow produces all blood cells, including lymphocytes.","rn":[["","What is the main job of red bone marrow?","What is the main job of red bone marrow?","Production of all blood cells; Including B and T lymphocytes"]]},{"id":36019,"t":"Immune System Tissues","s":"A child has a sore throat, and the doctor feels enlarged, tender lumps in the neck. Which explanation best fits?","o":["Lymph nodes enlarge as lymphocytes meet antigens from the infection","The thymus enlarges as T cells mature","The spleen swells as an emergency blood store","Red bone marrow expands into the neck","High endothelial venules leak blood into the tissues"],"e":"Lymph nodes are 1–25 mm egg-shaped or round structures found along lymphatic vessels, containing resident lymphocytes and macrophages that neutralise pathogens and clear debris. Many antigens from an infectious agent end up in the lymph nodes, where lymphocytes meet them and are trained to recognise them, so nodes become enlarged as a result of infection.","w":["The thymus matures T cells and is not the palpable neck swelling described.","The spleen is the emergency blood store and is not in the neck.","Bone marrow does not expand into the neck in infection.","HEVs are where lymphocytes cross from blood to lymph; they do not leak blood."],"k":"Lymph nodes (1–25 mm) enlarge in infection as lymphocytes meet antigen there.","rn":[["","Why are lymph nodes important for lymphocytes?","Why are lymph nodes important for lymphocytes?","Where they meet antigens; Trained to recognise them"]]},{"id":36020,"t":"Immune System Tissues","s":"Which pairing of immune tissue and role is NOT correct?","o":["Spleen: site of T-lymphocyte maturation","Red bone marrow: production of all blood cells","Lymph nodes: resident lymphocytes and macrophages","Thymus: maturation of T lymphocytes","Spleen: cleansing of blood from pathogens and debris"],"e":"The spleen acts as an emergency blood store and cleanses blood of pathogens and debris. T-lymphocyte maturation happens in the thymus. Red bone marrow produces all blood cells, and lymph nodes contain resident lymphocytes and macrophages that neutralise pathogens and clear debris.","w":["Red bone marrow does produce all blood cells, including B and T lymphocytes.","Lymph nodes do contain resident lymphocytes and macrophages.","The thymus is the site of T-lymphocyte maturation.","The spleen does cleanse blood of pathogens and debris."],"k":"Thymus matures T cells; spleen stores blood and cleanses it."},{"id":36021,"t":"Immune System Tissues","a":["thymus","the thymus"],"s":"Name the lymphoid organ where T lymphocytes mature.","e":"T cells emerge from the bone marrow as progenitors and then migrate to the thymus, where they mature. The thymus is one of the lymphoid tissues of the immune system, alongside lymph nodes and the spleen.","k":"T cells mature in the thymus.","rn":[["","Which 3 immune tissues are lymphoid?","Which 3 immune tissues are lymphoid?","Lymph nodes; Spleen; Thymus"],["","What are the 4 immune system tissues?","What are the 4 immune system tissues?","Red bone marrow; Lymph nodes; Spleen; Thymus"]]},{"id":36022,"t":"Soluble Factors","s":"Why is the immune system described as unusual in terms of the tissues it uses?","o":["It uses tissue components that also belong to other body systems","It has a single dedicated organ that makes every immune cell","Its cells are found only in the blood and never in tissues","It relies only on soluble molecules, not cells","All its tissues are part of the myeloid lineage"],"e":"The immune system uses tissue components that belong to other systems as well: the red bone marrow, the lymphoid system (lymph nodes), the spleen and the thymus. Its function also depends on both cells (leukocytes) and soluble factors. Bone marrow is myeloid, while lymph nodes, spleen and thymus are lymphoid.","w":["Several tissues are involved, not a single dedicated organ.","Immune cells move into tissues; some, such as dendritic cells and macrophages, are resident there.","Immune function relies on cells as well as soluble factors.","Lymph nodes, spleen and thymus are lymphoid, not myeloid."],"k":"The immune system shares tissues with other systems: marrow, lymph nodes, spleen, thymus.","rn":[["","What 2 components does immune system function rely on?","What 2 components does immune system function rely on?","Cells (leukocytes); Soluble factors"]]},{"id":36023,"t":"Leucocyte Lineages","s":"A drug selectively blocks the lymphoid progenitor in the bone marrow. Which cell type would still be produced normally?","o":["Neutrophils","B cells","Natural killer cells","Cytotoxic T cells","T helper cells"],"e":"The haematopoietic stem cell gives rise to a myeloid or a lymphoid progenitor. The myeloid lineage gives basophils, eosinophils, neutrophils, monocytes, macrophages, dendritic cells, platelets and erythrocytes. The lymphoid lineage gives cytotoxic T cells, T helper cells, B cells, dendritic cells and NK cells, so neutrophils would still be made.","w":["B cells come from the lymphoid progenitor.","NK cells come from the lymphoid progenitor.","Cytotoxic T cells come from the lymphoid progenitor via the thymus.","T helper cells come from the lymphoid progenitor via the thymus."],"k":"Neutrophils are myeloid; B, T and NK cells are lymphoid.","rn":[["","What leucocytes come from the myeloid lineage?","What leucocytes come from the myeloid lineage?","Basophils; Eosinophils; Neutrophils; Monocytes and macrophages"],["","Which progenitor gives rise to neutrophils?","Which progenitor gives rise to neutrophils?","Granulocyte-monocyte progenitor"]]},{"id":36024,"t":"Leucocyte Lineages","s":"Which statement about the haematopoietic stem cell is correct?","o":["It can divide to copy itself without committing to a lineage","It is found mainly in the thymus","It is already committed to the lymphoid lineage before any signal arrives","It differentiates without needing any molecular signal","It produces only red blood cells and platelets"],"e":"The haematopoietic stem cell in the bone marrow is non-differentiated and capable of division, producing copies of itself without committing to a lineage (self-renewing). Under appropriate conditions, triggered by molecular messengers such as cytokines binding to receptors, it differentiates into a myeloid or lymphoid progenitor. All blood cells start from it.","w":["It is in the bone marrow; the thymus is where T cells mature.","It is uncommitted until signalled to become myeloid or lymphoid.","Differentiation is triggered by messengers such as cytokines.","It gives rise to all blood cells, including white cells."],"k":"Haematopoietic stem cell: self-renewing, uncommitted, signalled by cytokines to become myeloid or lymphoid.","rn":[["","What is the haematopoietic stem cell like?","What is the haematopoietic stem cell like?","Undifferentiated and able to divide; Self-renews without committing to a lineage"]]},{"id":36025,"t":"Leucocyte Lineages","s":"Which cell type can arise from both the myeloid and the lymphoid lineages according to the lecture?","o":["Dendritic cell","Neutrophil","Erythrocyte","B cell","Mast cell"],"e":"The speaker notes list dendritic cells in both the myeloid lineage (with basophils, eosinophils, neutrophils, monocytes, macrophages, platelets and erythrocytes) and the lymphoid lineage (with cytotoxic T cells, T helper cells, B cells and NK cells). The lineage figure also shows dendritic cells on both sides.","w":["Neutrophils are myeloid only.","Erythrocytes are myeloid only.","B cells are lymphoid only.","Mast cells are shown on the myeloid side."],"k":"Dendritic cells appear in both lineages; other cells belong to one.","rn":[["","Which lineages give rise to dendritic cells?","Which lineages give rise to dendritic cells?","Both myeloid and lymphoid"],["","What cells come from the lymphoid lineage?","What cells come from the lymphoid lineage?","Cytotoxic T cells; T helper cells; B cells; Dendritic cells; NK cells"],["","What non-leucocyte cells also come from the myeloid lineage?","What non-leucocyte cells also come from the myeloid lineage?","Dendritic cells; Platelets; Erythrocytes"]]},{"id":36026,"t":"Leucocyte Lineages","s":"What triggers the haematopoietic stem cell to differentiate into a myeloid or lymphoid progenitor?","o":["Molecular messengers such as cytokines binding to its receptors","Contact with a pathogen in the lymph node","Migration of the stem cell into the thymus","Loss of its ability to divide","Release of histamine by nearby mast cells in the bone marrow"],"e":"Under appropriate conditions, the stem cell is triggered by specific molecular messengers, for example cytokines, binding to its receptors, and then differentiates into a myeloid or lymphoid progenitor. Cytokines are also used during infection to enhance differentiation in the bone marrow so more white cells are produced.","w":["The stem cell stays in the bone marrow; lymph nodes are where lymphocytes meet antigen.","T-cell progenitors, not stem cells, migrate to the thymus.","The stem cell keeps its ability to divide and self-renew.","Histamine is part of the allergic and inflammatory response."],"k":"Cytokines binding to receptors trigger stem cell differentiation.","rn":[["","What triggers a haematopoietic stem cell to differentiate?","What triggers a haematopoietic stem cell to differentiate?","Molecular messengers (e.g. cytokines); Bind to receptors on the stem cell"]]},{"id":36027,"t":"Cells of the Immune Response","s":"A blood test during an acute bacterial infection shows a sharp rise in one white cell type. It is a highly abundant, migratory phagocyte and granulocyte that coordinates the acute inflammatory response. Which cell is it?","o":["Neutrophil","Macrophage","Eosinophil","Dendritic cell","Mast cell"],"e":"Neutrophils are phagocytes and granulocytes, highly abundant and migratory, and coordinate the acute inflammatory response. In infection the bone marrow starts producing larger amounts of them. Macrophages are phagocytes but not granulocytes; eosinophils target parasites; dendritic cells present antigen; mast cells facilitate inflammation but are not phagocytes.","w":["Macrophages are phagocytes and antigen presenters but are not granulocytes.","Eosinophils defend against nematodes and other parasites.","Dendritic cells are antigen presenting cells, not abundant phagocytic granulocytes.","Mast cells are granulocytes that facilitate inflammation but are not the abundant phagocyte."],"k":"Neutrophil: abundant migratory phagocytic granulocyte coordinating acute inflammation.","rn":[["","What are the key features of neutrophils?","What are the key features of neutrophils?","Phagocyte, granulocyte; Highly abundant and migratory; Coordinates acute inflammatory response"]]},{"id":36028,"t":"Cells of the Immune Response","s":"A traveller has a nematode infection, and blood shows a raised level of a granulocyte specialised for host defence against nematodes and other parasites. Which cell is raised?","o":["Eosinophil","Neutrophil","Macrophage","Mast cell","Natural killer cell"],"e":"Eosinophils are granulocytes involved in host defence against nematodes and other parasites. Basophils are also involved in defence against multicellular parasites. Neutrophils coordinate acute inflammation, macrophages phagocytose and present antigen, and mast cells facilitate acute inflammation.","w":["Neutrophils coordinate the acute inflammatory response to infection generally.","Macrophages are not granulocytes.","Mast cells facilitate the acute inflammatory response, not specifically nematode defence.","NK cells are lymphoid cells, not granulocytes."],"k":"Eosinophils defend against nematodes and other parasites.","rn":[["","What is the main job of eosinophils?","What is the main job of eosinophils?","Defence against nematodes and other parasites"]]},{"id":36029,"t":"Cells of the Immune Response","s":"Which innate cell type is BOTH a phagocyte AND an antigen presenting cell?","o":["Macrophage","Neutrophil","Dendritic cell","Basophil","Mast cell"],"e":"Macrophages are phagocytes, highly migratory and antigen presenting: they digest a pathogen, keep components (antigens) and present them to other immune cells. Neutrophils are phagocytes but not listed as antigen presenters. Dendritic cells present antigen but are not listed as phagocytes. Basophils and mast cells are granulocytes.","w":["Neutrophils are phagocytes but are not antigen presenting cells.","Dendritic cells present antigen but are not listed as phagocytes on the slide.","Basophils are granulocytes for defence against multicellular parasites.","Mast cells are granulocytes that facilitate inflammation."],"k":"Macrophage = phagocyte + antigen presenting cell.","rn":[["","Which 2 innate cells are phagocytes?","Which 2 innate cells are phagocytes?","Macrophage; Neutrophil"],["","What are the 6 cells of the innate immune response?","What are the 6 cells of the innate immune response?","Macrophage; Neutrophil; Eosinophil; Basophil; Mast cell; Dendritic cell"]]},{"id":36030,"t":"Cells of the Immune Response","s":"Which of the following is NOT a granulocyte?","o":["Macrophage","Neutrophil","Eosinophil","Basophil","Mast cell"],"e":"Granulocytes are leucocytes that contain granules of digestive enzymes and molecules involved in the acute immune response and inflammation. The slide lists neutrophil, eosinophil, basophil and mast cell as granulocytes. The macrophage is a phagocyte and antigen presenting cell, not a granulocyte.","w":["Neutrophils are listed as granulocytes.","Eosinophils are listed as granulocytes.","Basophils are listed as granulocytes.","Mast cells are listed as granulocytes."],"k":"Granulocytes: neutrophil, eosinophil, basophil, mast cell.","rn":[["","What are the 6 cells of the innate immune response?","What are the 6 cells of the innate immune response?","Macrophage; Neutrophil; Eosinophil; Basophil; Mast cell; Dendritic cell"]]},{"id":36031,"t":"Cells of the Immune Response","s":"A skin biopsy shows a resident cell that captures a pathogen molecule, holds on to it and later shows it to other immune cells so they are prepared when they meet it. It is not listed as a phagocyte. Which cell is it?","o":["Dendritic cell","Neutrophil that has migrated from the circulating blood","Eosinophil responding to a nematode infection","Basophil defending against a multicellular parasite","Mast cell facilitating the acute inflammatory response"],"e":"Dendritic cells are antigen presenting cells. They are migratory but also resident in tissues such as the skin; they keep hold of an antigen from a pathogen and present it to other immune cells, which then learn about it. The slide lists them as antigen presenting cells only, whereas macrophages are both phagocytes and antigen presenters.","w":["Neutrophils are phagocytes that coordinate acute inflammation; they are not listed as antigen presenters.","Eosinophils are granulocytes for defence against nematodes and parasites.","Basophils are granulocytes for defence against multicellular parasites.","Mast cells are granulocytes that facilitate inflammation, not antigen presenters."],"k":"Dendritic cells: tissue-resident and migratory antigen presenting cells.","rn":[["","Why do dendritic cells hold and present an antigen?","Why do dendritic cells hold and present an antigen?","Other immune cells learn about it; So they are prepared when they meet it"],["","Where are dendritic cells found?","Where are dendritic cells found?","Resident in tissues (e.g. skin)"]]},{"id":36032,"t":"Cells of the Immune Response","a":["granulocyte","granulocytes"],"s":"What is the general name for leucocytes that contain granules of digestive enzymes and molecules involved in the acute immune response and inflammation?","e":"Granulocytes contain granules with digestive enzymes and molecules involved in the acute immune response and inflammation. The lecture's granulocytes are neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils and mast cells.","k":"Granulocytes: granule-containing leucocytes (neutrophil, eosinophil, basophil, mast cell).","rn":[["","What are the key features of neutrophils?","What are the key features of neutrophils?","Phagocyte, granulocyte; Highly abundant and migratory; Coordinates acute inflammatory response"],["","Which progenitor gives rise to neutrophils?","Which progenitor gives rise to neutrophils?","Granulocyte-monocyte progenitor"]]},{"id":36033,"t":"Cells of the Immune Response","s":"A patient lacks functional B cells but has normal T cells. Which function would be most directly lost?","o":["Production of antibodies that bind antigens on pathogens","Killing of virus-infected cells by lymphocytes","Coordination of the activation and termination of immune responses by lymphocytes","Phagocytosis of bacteria at the site of inflammation","Release of histamine during an allergic reaction"],"e":"B cells are adaptive cells that produce antibodies that bind to antigens on pathogens, and they show immunological memory. T cells kill virus-infected cells and coordinate immune responses, acting as orchestrators of activation and termination. Phagocytosis is done by innate cells such as macrophages and neutrophils, and histamine comes from mast cells.","w":["Killing virus-infected cells is a T cell function.","Orchestrating activation and termination is a T cell function.","Phagocytosis is performed by innate phagocytes.","Histamine release is from mast cells in allergy."],"k":"B cells make antibodies and show memory; T cells kill infected cells and coordinate.","rn":[["","What is the main job of B cells?","What is the main job of B cells?","Produce antibodies that bind antigens on pathogens"]]},{"id":36034,"t":"Cells of the Immune Response","s":"Which statement best describes T cells as presented in this lecture?","o":["They kill virus-infected cells and orchestrate the activation and termination of responses","They produce antibodies that bind antigens on pathogens","They are granulocytes that coordinate acute inflammation","They are innate phagocytes that present antigen","They mature in the spleen, which also acts as the emergency blood store of the body"],"e":"T cells are adaptive cells involved in killing virus-infected cells and in coordinating immune responses: they are the orchestrators of activation and termination. They mature in the thymus. Antibody production belongs to B cells, and granulocytes and phagocytes are innate cells.","w":["Antibody production is the function of B cells.","Granulocytes such as neutrophils are innate cells, not T cells.","Macrophages are the innate phagocytes that present antigen.","T cells mature in the thymus; the spleen is the emergency blood store."],"k":"T cells: kill virus-infected cells and orchestrate activation/termination.","rn":[["","T cells orchestrate what?","T cells orchestrate what?","Activation and termination of immune responses"],["","What are the 2 jobs of T cells?","What are the 2 jobs of T cells?","Killing virus-infected cells; Coordinating immune responses"]]},{"id":36035,"t":"Lymphocyte Recirculation","s":"Why do B and T lymphocytes continually circulate between the blood and the lymphatic system?","o":["To maximise the chance that committed lymphocytes meet their antigen","To deliver oxygen to tissues in the lymph","To return to the bone marrow and divide","To avoid contact with antigen until they are mature","Because the antibodies they produce are confined to the bloodstream and cannot reach tissues"],"e":"Continual recirculation allows the maximum number of antigenically committed lymphocytes to encounter and interact with antigen. Only about 1 in 100,000 lymphocytes binds a particular antigen, so each must travel widely to stand a chance of detecting it. B cells, and their antibodies, can access all areas: antibodies are not restricted to the bloodstream.","w":["Lymphocytes are immune cells; they do not carry oxygen.","Recirculation is surveillance for antigen, not a return to divide in the marrow.","Lymphocytes circulate precisely to find antigen, not to avoid it.","Antibodies are NOT restricted to the bloodstream."],"k":"Recirculation maximises the chance of committed lymphocytes meeting their antigen.","rn":[["","Why does recirculation matter for immunological memory?","Why does recirculation matter for immunological memory?","Trained lymphocytes can meet the same antigen again"],["","What is the purpose of lymphocyte recirculation?","What is the purpose of lymphocyte recirculation?","Maximum lymphocytes encounter antigen"],["","Why are lymph nodes important for lymphocytes?","Why are lymph nodes important for lymphocytes?","Where they meet antigens; Trained to recognise them"]]},{"id":36036,"t":"Lymphocyte Recirculation","s":"Approximately what fraction of lymphocytes will bind any one particular antigen?","o":["About 1 in 100,000","About 1 in 100","About 1 in 1,000","About 1 in 10","About half"],"e":"About 1 in 100,000 lymphocytes will bind to a particular antigen. Because so few cells match, lymphocytes must travel widely, circuiting the blood and lymphatic system 1 to 2 times a day, to stand a chance of detecting their antigen. This is why recirculation and HEV function matter so much.","w":["1 in 100 overstates the frequency by a thousand-fold.","1 in 1,000 overstates the frequency by a hundred-fold.","1 in 10 is far too common for a single antigen.","Half the lymphocytes would leave no specificity at all."],"k":"~1 in 100,000 lymphocytes binds a given antigen.","rn":[["","Why must each lymphocyte travel widely?","Why must each lymphocyte travel widely?","Only 1 in 100,000 binds an antigen"]]},{"id":36037,"t":"Lymphocyte Recirculation","s":"A drug prevents lymphocytes from crossing the walls of the high endothelial venules (HEVs). Where would the block act?","o":["In lymph nodes, where lymphocytes pass from blood into lymphatic tissue","In the thymus, where T cells mature","In the bone marrow, where blood cells are made","In the spleen, where blood is stored for emergencies and cleansed of pathogens","At the skin surface, the first physical barrier"],"e":"HEVs are sites in lymph nodes where lymphocytes cross from blood to the lymphatic system. They are lined with tall endothelial cells that give a thickened appearance and are organised to help lymphocytes pass through the venule walls. Before a lymphocyte can enter peripheral lymphoid organs it must adhere to and penetrate the endothelial layer.","w":["The thymus is the maturation site for T cells, not the HEV crossing point.","The bone marrow is the production site of blood cells.","The spleen is an emergency blood store, not the site of HEVs.","Skin is a barrier, not a site of HEVs."],"k":"HEVs in lymph nodes: where lymphocytes cross from blood to lymph.","rn":[["","What are High Endothelial Venules (HEVs)?","What are High Endothelial Venules (HEVs)?","Sites in lymph nodes where lymphocytes cross from blood to lymphatic system"]]},{"id":36038,"t":"Lymphocyte Recirculation","a":["high endothelial venule","high endothelial venules","hev","hevs"],"s":"Name the specialised vessels in lymph nodes, lined by tall endothelial cells, through which lymphocytes leave the blood.","e":"High endothelial venules (HEVs) are the sites in lymph nodes where lymphocytes cross from blood to the lymphatic system. Their tall endothelial cells give them a thickened appearance and help lymphocytes pass through the venule wall.","k":"HEVs: tall-endothelium venules in lymph nodes for lymphocyte entry.","rn":[["","What are High Endothelial Venules (HEVs)?","What are High Endothelial Venules (HEVs)?","Sites in lymph nodes where lymphocytes cross from blood to lymphatic system"],["","What does HEVs stand for?","What does HEVs stand for?","High Endothelial Venules"]]},{"id":36039,"t":"Lymphocyte Recirculation","s":"Which statement about lymphocyte recirculation is NOT correct?","o":["Antibodies made by B cells are restricted to the bloodstream","An individual lymphocyte may circuit the system 1 to 2 times a day","Lymphocytes must adhere to and penetrate endothelium to enter tissues","Recirculation helps lymphocytes meet antigens they have met before","Lymphocytes return through lymphatics to the thoracic duct and the blood"],"e":"B cells can access all areas, and so can the antibodies they produce: antibodies are NOT restricted to the bloodstream. A lymphocyte circuits 1 to 2 times a day, must adhere to and penetrate endothelium to enter inflamed tissue or lymphoid organs, and returns via afferent and efferent lymphatics to the thoracic duct. Recirculation is a key step in immunological memory.","w":["Circuiting 1 to 2 times a day is stated in the lecture.","Adhering to and penetrating endothelium is required before entry to tissues.","Recirculation lets lymphocytes meet antigens again when they re-enter the body.","The recirculation pathway returns lymphocytes via the thoracic duct to the blood."],"k":"Antibodies, like B cells, can reach all areas: they are not restricted to blood.","rn":[["","Are antibodies restricted to the bloodstream?","Are antibodies restricted to the bloodstream?","No; Access all areas (e.g. lymphatics)"]]},{"id":36040,"t":"Lymphocyte Recirculation","s":"In the lymph node figure, lymph arrives at the node through one vessel and leaves through another. Which pair is correct?","o":["Arrives via afferent lymphatic; leaves via efferent lymphatic","Arrives via efferent lymphatic; leaves via afferent lymphatic","Arrives via the HEV; leaves via the artery","Arrives via the thoracic duct; leaves via the HEV","Arrives via the vein; leaves via the afferent lymphatic"],"e":"In the lymph node figure, the afferent lymphatic enters at the top and the efferent lymphatic leaves at the bottom right. The artery and vein enter at the bottom, and the HEV is the venule where lymphocytes leave the blood. The recirculation pathway returns lymphocytes through afferent and efferent lymphatics to the thoracic duct and back into the blood.","w":["This reverses the directions: afferent brings lymph in, efferent takes it out.","The HEV is where lymphocytes leave the blood, and arteries bring blood in.","The thoracic duct receives lymph from the lymphatics on the way back to the blood.","Blood vessels supply the node; the afferent lymphatic brings lymph in."],"k":"Lymph enters a node via afferent lymphatics and leaves via efferent lymphatics.","f":"im15"},{"id":36041,"t":"Pattern Recognition Receptors","s":"A macrophage binds a lipopolysaccharide molecule from a Gram-negative bacterium. Which description of this interaction is correct?","o":["A pattern recognition receptor binds a pathogen associated molecular pattern","An antibody made by a B cell binds a specific epitope on a self antigen of the host","A T cell receptor binds a damage associated molecular pattern","Complement binds a Toll-like receptor on a B cell","Lysozyme binds peptidoglycan on the outer membrane"],"e":"Pattern recognition receptors (PRRs) in innate immunity detect antigens non-specifically by identifying pathogen associated molecular patterns (PAMPs). PAMPs occur in lower organisms but not in humans; examples are lipopolysaccharides in the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria and peptidoglycans on the Gram-positive wall. PRRs sit on innate cells such as macrophages and dendritic cells.","w":["Antibodies are adaptive and do not bind self antigens in health; LPS is a PAMP.","DAMPs are patterns from damaged cells, and the macrophage uses a PRR.","Complement is a soluble factor, and Toll-like receptors are PRRs on innate cells.","Peptidoglycan is on the Gram-positive wall; lipopolysaccharide is in the Gram-negative outer membrane."],"k":"PRRs on innate cells recognise PAMPs such as LPS and peptidoglycan.","rn":[["","What does PRR (pattern recognition receptor) binding to a PAMP (pathogen associated mol…","What does PRR (pattern recognition receptor) binding to a PAMP (pathogen associated molecular pattern) lead to?","Destruction of the pathogen; Signalling to other immune cells"]]},{"id":36042,"t":"Pattern Recognition Receptors","s":"Why do PAMPs make good targets for innate immune recognition?","o":["They occur in lower organisms but not in humans","They are found on every human cell surface","They change quickly with every new infection","They are recognised only after antibody binding","They appear only on dead host cells"],"e":"PAMPs are molecular structures that occur in lower organisms but not in humans, so recognising them lets PRRs detect pathogens without attacking self. Examples include lipopolysaccharides and peptidoglycans. Patterns from damaged host cells are a separate group, the DAMPs, covered in another session.","w":["If PAMPs were on human cells, recognising them would attack self.","PRRs bind a limited, fixed set of patterns, not changing ones.","PRRs bind PAMPs directly without antibodies.","Patterns from damaged cells are DAMPs, not PAMPs."],"k":"PAMPs are present in lower organisms but absent from humans.","rn":[["","What are PAMPs?","What are PAMPs?","Molecular structures in lower organisms, not in humans"]]},{"id":36043,"t":"Pattern Recognition Receptors","a":["toll-like receptors","toll like receptors","tlr","tlrs","toll-like receptor"],"s":"Name the large family of pattern recognition receptors given as the main example in this lecture.","e":"Toll-like receptors are a large family of pattern recognition receptors (PRRs). They recognise pathogen associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) on innate cells such as macrophages and dendritic cells. They are covered further in the sessions on inflammation.","k":"Toll-like receptors are a large PRR family.","rn":[["","What is the large family of PRRs?","What is the large family of PRRs?","Toll-like receptors"]]},{"id":36044,"t":"Pattern Recognition Receptors","s":"A peptidoglycan PAMP is recognised by a phagocyte. Which bacterial structure most likely carried it?","o":["The cell wall of a Gram-positive bacterium","The outer membrane of a Gram-negative bacterium","The nucleus of a human epithelial cell","The membrane attack complex","A human self antigen in the gut"],"e":"Peptidoglycans are found abundantly on the bacterial cell wall, and the speaker notes link them to Gram-positive bacteria. Lipopolysaccharides are found in the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria. PAMPs occur in lower organisms, not in human cells.","w":["The Gram-negative outer membrane carries lipopolysaccharide.","Human cells do not carry PAMPs.","The MAC is a host complement structure that punches holes in pathogens.","Self antigens are tolerated and are not PAMPs."],"k":"Peptidoglycan = Gram-positive cell wall PAMP; LPS = Gram-negative outer membrane PAMP.","rn":[["","Where are peptidoglycans found?","Where are peptidoglycans found?","Gram-positive bacterial cell wall"]]},{"id":36045,"t":"Pattern Recognition Receptors","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts pattern recognition receptors with the receptors of adaptive immunity?","o":["PRRs have a restricted repertoire and give no memory","PRRs bind only protein peptides, while adaptive receptors bind lipids","PRRs have a broader repertoire than B and T cell receptors","PRRs make cells respond faster on a second meeting with a pathogen","PRRs are modified during life, while adaptive receptors are fixed by genes"],"e":"PRRs have a much more restricted binding repertoire than adaptive receptors and give no immunological memory: innate cells are not trained to respond faster on a second meeting. They bind PAMPs that may be proteins, carbohydrates or lipids and are set by our genes. Adaptive receptors bind primarily protein peptides (sometimes carbohydrates and lipids for B cells) and have memory.","w":["PRRs bind proteins, carbohydrates or lipids; adaptive receptors bind mainly protein peptides.","Adaptive receptors have the broader repertoire.","Memory and faster second responses belong to adaptive immunity.","PRRs are set by our genes; adaptive receptors can be modified."],"k":"PRRs: restricted, gene-set, no memory; adaptive receptors: broad, modifiable, with memory.","rn":[["","PRR vs adaptive receptors: repertoire","PRR vs adaptive receptors: repertoire","PRR: much more restricted; Adaptive: broad"]]},{"id":36046,"t":"Pattern Recognition Receptors","s":"A virus has already entered a macrophage, and its genetic material is detected inside the cell. Where are the receptors that detect it most likely located?","o":["Intracellular PRRs in endosomes","Antibodies on the surface of the cell","T cell receptors on the macrophage membrane","HEVs inside the cytoplasm","Complement proteins in the nucleus"],"e":"PRRs are found on the cell membrane, for extracellular pathogens or ones about to infect, and intracellularly in endosomes. Endosomal PRRs can recognise pathogen components such as genetic material in the process of infecting the cell, after entry, or as they are degraded inside it.","w":["Antibodies are made by B cells and do not detect genetic material inside a macrophage.","T cell receptors belong to T cells, not macrophages.","HEVs are blood vessels in lymph nodes, not intracellular structures.","Complement circulates in plasma and is not a nuclear sensor."],"k":"PRRs occur on the cell membrane and intracellularly in endosomes.","rn":[["","Where are PRRs found in the cell?","Where are PRRs found in the cell?","Cell membrane; Intracellularly in endosomes"],["","What do PRRs (pattern recognition receptors) in endosomes detect?","What do PRRs (pattern recognition receptors) in endosomes detect?","Pathogen components entering the cell (e.g. genetic material); Components being degraded in the cell"]]},{"id":36047,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","s":"Why does a phagocyte need PAMP binding to cross-link its receptors before phagocytosis starts?","o":["Cross-linking brings receptor enzymes together so they can phosphorylate each other","Each receptor enzyme phosphorylates itself once it is bound by any single PAMP","Cross-linking releases lysozyme from the bacterial surface","The membrane must fuse with a lysosome before receptors can bind","Cross-linking stops the cell producing cytokines"],"e":"The intracellular domains of the phagocyte's receptors carry enzymes that can phosphorylate others of their kind, but not themselves. Normally the membrane keeps these domains apart; PAMP-mediated cross-linking drags the receptor complexes together, phosphorylation occurs and a signal transduction pathway triggers phagocytosis. This two-receptor requirement protects against overreaction or a non-specific response.","w":["The enzymes cannot phosphorylate themselves; they phosphorylate neighbouring receptors.","Lysozyme is a host enzyme in tears and saliva, not released by cross-linking.","Lysosome fusion happens after ingestion, not before binding.","Phagocytosis often comes with release of soluble molecules that activate other immune cells."],"k":"PAMP cross-linking → mutual phosphorylation of receptor enzymes → phagocytosis.","rn":[["","What does PAMP-mediated cross-linking do?","What does PAMP-mediated cross-linking do?","Drags receptor complexes together; Phosphorylation occurs"]]},{"id":36048,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","s":"Put the steps of phagocytosis in order: (P) phagosome fuses with lysosome; (Q) bacterium attaches to pseudopodia; (R) digestion products are released; (S) bacterium is ingested forming a phagosome; (T) bacterium is killed and digested by lysosomal enzymes.","o":["Q → S → P → T → R","S → Q → P → T → R","Q → P → S → T → R","Q → S → T → P → R","P → Q → S → T → R"],"e":"The five steps are: (1) the bacterium becomes attached to membrane evaginations called pseudopodia; (2) it is ingested, forming a phagosome; (3) the phagosome fuses with a lysosome; (4) the bacterium is killed and digested by lysosomal enzymes; (5) digestion products are released from the cell.","w":["Attachment must come before ingestion.","The phagosome must form before it can fuse with a lysosome.","Killing by lysosomal enzymes needs lysosome fusion first.","Lysosome fusion cannot happen before the bacterium is attached and ingested."],"k":"Attach → ingest (phagosome) → lysosome fusion → kill/digest → release.","rn":[["","What are the 5 steps of phagocytosis?","What are the 5 steps of phagocytosis?","Bacterium attaches to pseudopodia; Ingested, forming phagosome; Phagosome fuses with lysosome; Bacterium killed and digested; Digestion products released"]]},{"id":36049,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","s":"Within a phagocyte, a structure forms when the vesicle containing an ingested bacterium fuses with a lysosome. What is it called?","o":["Phagolysosome","Phagosome","Pseudopodium","Endosome","Granule"],"e":"After attachment to pseudopodia, the ingested bacterium sits in a phagosome. The phagosome then fuses with a lysosome (granule fusion), completing formation of the phagolysosome, where lysosomal enzymes kill and digest the bacterium. Microbial products are then released.","w":["The phagosome is the vesicle before lysosome fusion.","Pseudopodia are the membrane extensions that attach to the bacterium.","Endosomes are where intracellular PRRs are found.","Granules fuse with the phagosome but the combined structure is the phagolysosome."],"k":"Phagosome + lysosome = phagolysosome."},{"id":36050,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","s":"Which sequence correctly describes the three steps of leucocyte signalling you need for this lecture?","o":["Ligand–receptor interaction → phosphorylation of adapter molecules → initiation of gene expression","Gene expression → ligand release → receptor phosphorylation","Phosphorylation of adapter molecules → ligand–receptor interaction → initiation of gene expression","Ligand–receptor interaction → initiation of gene expression → phosphorylation of adapter molecules","Initiation of gene expression → phosphorylation of adapter molecules → ligand–receptor interaction"],"e":"A ligand (a small signalling molecule or a PAMP) binds its receptor; the receptor-associated adapter molecules are phosphorylated (leucocyte receptors often rely on adapters); a cascade runs down through the cytoplasm to the nucleus and initiates gene expression, altering cell function. The individual molecules in the cascade are not needed for this session.","w":["Gene expression is the end of the cascade, not the start.","Phosphorylation of adapters follows binding of the ligand.","Gene expression comes after adapter phosphorylation.","This sequence is completely reversed."],"k":"Ligand–receptor → adapter phosphorylation → gene expression.","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of leucocyte signalling?","What are the 3 steps of leucocyte signalling?","Ligand-receptor interaction; Phosphorylation of adapter molecules; Initiation of gene expression"]]},{"id":36051,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","s":"Which statement about ligand–receptor communication between immune cells is NOT correct?","o":["The ligand must always enter the target cell to reach its receptor","The interaction is specific and of moderate to high affinity","The ligand may be anchored on the sending cell's surface","Binding usually triggers a cascade that changes gene expression","Some receptors are intracellular for ligands that cross the membrane"],"e":"Immune cells communicate when a ligand binds a receptor on the target cell. The interaction is specific and typically of moderate to high affinity. The receptor is usually on the cell surface, occasionally intracellular for ligands that cross the membrane, and sometimes the ligand is anchored on a cell surface, requiring close contact. Binding typically induces a cascade that changes gene expression and so cell function.","w":["Specific, moderate-to-high-affinity binding is stated.","Ligands anchored on the sender's surface require close proximity, as stated.","Changes in gene expression are the usual outcome.","Intracellular receptors exist for ligands that can pass through the membrane."],"k":"Receptors are usually on the cell surface; only some ligands enter the cell.","rn":[["","Where can leucocyte receptors be located?","Where can leucocyte receptors be located?","Cell surface; Occasionally intracellular (membrane-permeable ligands)"],["","How do cytokines change cell function?","How do cytokines change cell function?","Bind target receptors; Signalling cascade; Changes in gene expression"],["","How specific and strong is ligand-receptor binding on immune cells?","How specific and strong is ligand-receptor binding on immune cells?","Specific; Moderate to high affinity"]]},{"id":36052,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","a":["affinity"],"s":"What is the term for the strength of binding between a ligand and its receptor?","e":"Affinity is the strength of binding. Ligand–receptor interactions between immune cells are specific and typically of moderate to high affinity, and binding typically induces a signal transduction cascade.","k":"Affinity = strength of binding.","rn":[["","What is affinity?","What is affinity?","Strength of binding"]]},{"id":36053,"t":"Inflammatory Response","s":"A footballer cuts his knee. Within minutes the area becomes painful, red and swollen. Which early event explains the pain and the histamine release?","o":["Damaged tissue releases bradykinin, causing pain and stimulating mast cells","Neutrophils release lysozyme into the wound, which causes the pain directly","Macrophages present antigen to B cells in the knee","Complement forms the MAC on the player's own cells","HEVs open in the skin to release lymphocytes"],"e":"Damaged tissues rupture and release bradykinin, which causes the sensation of pain and stimulates mast cells to release histamine. Capillaries then dilate and become permeable, and local temperature rises. Neutrophils migrate in, monocytes become macrophages, and macrophages secrete factors that recruit more neutrophils.","w":["Lysozyme is a barrier enzyme in tears and saliva, not the trigger of pain.","Antigen presentation is not the early event causing pain and histamine release.","The MAC targets invading pathogens, not the cause of early pain.","HEVs are in lymph nodes, not the skin of the knee."],"k":"Bradykinin → pain + mast cell histamine release.","rn":[["","What do damaged tissues release that causes pain?","What do damaged tissues release that causes pain?","Bradykinin"]]},{"id":36054,"t":"Inflammatory Response","s":"Which statement about the inflammatory response is correct?","o":["Monocytes leave permeable capillaries and mature into macrophages","Capillaries constrict and become less permeable","Local temperature falls at the site of damage","Neutrophils leave the tissue and return to the blood","Macrophages secrete protein factors that reduce further neutrophil recruitment to the site"],"e":"In inflammation, capillaries dilate and become permeable and local temperature rises. Neutrophils migrate to the damaged area and destroy bacteria. Monocytes escape from the permeable capillaries and mature into macrophages, which engulf invaders and secrete protein factors that recruit more neutrophils.","w":["Capillaries dilate and become more permeable.","Local temperature rises.","Neutrophils migrate into the damaged area.","Macrophage factors recruit more neutrophils."],"k":"Inflammation: dilated permeable capillaries, heat, neutrophils in, monocytes → macrophages.","rn":[["","What do monocytes do in inflammation?","What do monocytes do in inflammation?","Escape permeable capillaries; Mature into macrophages"]]},{"id":36055,"t":"Inflammatory Response","s":"In the inflammation figure, phagocytes move from the capillary towards bacteria in damaged tissue. What is the name for this directed movement in response to chemical signals?","o":["Chemotaxis","Exudate formation","Opsonisation","Haematopoiesis","Juxtacrine signalling"],"e":"The figure shows four steps: tissue damage releases vasoactive and chemotactic factors that increase blood flow and capillary permeability; fluid and cells (exudate) flood in; phagocytes migrate to the site of inflammation (chemotaxis); and phagocytes with the antibacterial exudate destroy the bacteria. Neutrophils are chemo-attracted to the site.","w":["Exudate is the fluid and cells that flood in, not the cell movement itself.","The coating term is garbled in the recording; the movement of phagocytes is chemotaxis.","Haematopoiesis is blood cell production in the bone marrow.","Juxtacrine signalling needs direct cell contact and is not migration."],"k":"Chemotaxis = migration of phagocytes to the site of inflammation.","f":"im21","rn":[["","What is chemotaxis?","What is chemotaxis?","Phagocytes migrating to the site of inflammation"]]},{"id":36056,"t":"Inflammatory Response","s":"Which component is listed in the inflammatory exudate in the lecture figure?","o":["Complement and C-reactive protein","Bradykinin and red bone marrow","High endothelial venules","Thymic T-cell progenitors","Toll-like receptors released into fluid"],"e":"The inflammation figure labels the exudate as containing complement and C-reactive protein. Permeable capillaries allow an influx of fluid and cells, and phagocytes together with this antibacterial exudate destroy the bacteria. Soluble molecules such as complement take part in the response.","w":["Bradykinin is released by damaged tissue; marrow is a tissue, not exudate.","HEVs are vessels in lymph nodes.","T-cell progenitors are in the thymus.","Toll-like receptors are on cells, not free in exudate."],"k":"Inflammatory exudate contains complement and C-reactive protein.","rn":[["","What does inflammatory exudate contain?","What does inflammatory exudate contain?","Complement; C-reactive protein"]]},{"id":36057,"t":"Inflammatory Response","s":"Which statement about the role of capillaries in inflammation is correct?","o":["They become permeable, letting fluid and leucocytes such as neutrophils into the tissue","They become impermeable to fluid, trapping neutrophils and other leucocytes inside the blood","They produce new red blood cells to replace lost ones","They release HEVs into the damaged tissue","They make lysozyme to break down bacterial walls"],"e":"Vasoactive and chemotactic factors cause a local increase in blood flow and capillary permeability. Permeable capillaries allow an influx of fluid (exudate) and cells, and capillary changes let leucocytes such as neutrophils move from the blood into the tissues, where they are chemo-attracted to the site.","w":["Capillaries become more permeable, not less.","Blood cell production happens in the red bone marrow.","HEVs are a type of venule found in lymph nodes.","Lysozyme is in tears, nasal secretions and saliva."],"k":"Permeable capillaries let exudate and neutrophils into inflamed tissue.","rn":[["","What is exudate?","What is exudate?","Fluid and cells entering tissue from permeable capillaries"]]},{"id":36058,"t":"Inflammatory Response","a":["bradykinin"],"s":"Name the substance released from ruptured damaged tissue that causes pain and stimulates mast cells to release histamine.","e":"Damaged tissues rupture and release bradykinin, which results in the sensation of pain and stimulates mast cells to release histamine. Capillaries then dilate and become permeable, and local temperature rises.","k":"Bradykinin: pain + mast cell histamine release.","rn":[["","What do damaged tissues release that causes pain?","What do damaged tissues release that causes pain?","Bradykinin"],["","What does bradykinin stimulate?","What does bradykinin stimulate?","Mast cells to release histamine"]]},{"id":36059,"t":"Soluble Factors","s":"A patient has an inherited deficiency of a late complement protein, so the membrane attack complex (MAC) cannot form. Which complement function is most directly lost?","o":["Punching holes in invading bacterial membranes, causing lysis","Attracting phagocytes to the site of infection","Coating bacteria to mark them for destruction","Releasing bradykinin from the damaged tissue at the site of infection","Breaking glycosidic bonds of the bacterial cell wall"],"e":"Complement is a family of plasma proteins that activate each other in a cascade. They destabilise the membranes of invading bacteria, coat bacteria to mark them for destruction, and attract phagocytes. The cascade forms the MAC, which inserts into pathogen membranes and punches holes that lead to lysis, so this is the function lost.","w":["Attracting phagocytes is a separate complement function not dependent on the MAC.","Coating bacteria is a separate complement function.","Bradykinin comes from damaged tissue, not complement.","Glycosidic bond breakage is the action of lysozyme."],"k":"The MAC inserts into pathogen membranes and punches holes, causing lysis.","rn":[["","What does the Membrane Attack Complex do?","What does the Membrane Attack Complex do?","Punches holes in pathogen membrane; Causes lysis"]]},{"id":36060,"t":"Soluble Factors","s":"Which of the following is NOT a function of complement described in the lecture?","o":["Interfering with viral replication inside host cells","Destabilising the membranes of invading bacteria","Coating bacteria to mark them for destruction","Attracting phagocytes to the site of infection","Activating other complement proteins in a cascade"],"e":"Complement proteins activate each other (the complement cascade), destabilise bacterial membranes, coat bacteria marking them for destruction by antibodies or phagocytosis, and attract phagocytes. Interfering with viral replication is the role of interferon, a cytokine.","w":["Destabilising bacterial membranes is listed.","Coating bacteria for destruction is listed.","Attracting phagocytes is listed.","Activation of each other in a cascade is listed."],"k":"Interferon, not complement, interferes with viral replication.","rn":[["","What is interferon?","What is interferon?","A glycoprotein cytokine; Interferes with viral replication"]]},{"id":36061,"t":"Soluble Factors","s":"A child with a viral infection produces a glycoprotein that interferes with viral replication. Which soluble factor is this?","o":["Interferon","Lysozyme, the hydrolytic enzyme found in tears and saliva","Complement, the cascade of plasma proteins","Histamine released from activated mast cells","Bradykinin released from ruptured damaged tissue"],"e":"Interferon is a cytokine: a glycoprotein that interferes with viral replication. Cytokines are small, highly mobile proteins and the primary signalling molecules of the immune system; interleukins and interferons are examples, and interferons are involved in the fight against viruses.","w":["Lysozyme destroys the bacterial cell wall; it does not interfere with viral replication.","Complement destabilises and lyses bacteria; it is not the anti-replication glycoprotein.","Histamine is released by mast cells in allergy and inflammation.","Bradykinin causes pain and stimulates histamine release."],"k":"Interferon: a cytokine glycoprotein that interferes with viral replication.","rn":[["","What is interferon?","What is interferon?","A glycoprotein cytokine; Interferes with viral replication"]]},{"id":36062,"t":"Soluble Factors","s":"Which statement about chemokines is correct?","o":["They are a subset of cytokines that attract cells to the site of infection","They are complement proteins that form the membrane attack complex in bacteria","They are enzymes in saliva that break glycosidic bonds in the bacterial wall","They are receptors on macrophages that recognise pathogen patterns","They are large proteins that cannot move from the cell that makes them"],"e":"Chemokines are a subset of cytokines, named because they are chemo-attracting cytokines: they attract cells to the site of infection. Cytokines are small, highly mobile proteins and the primary signalling molecules of the immune system. Leucocytes communicate using these small soluble messaging proteins.","w":["The MAC is formed by the complement cascade, not chemokines.","Breaking glycosidic bonds is the action of lysozyme.","Receptors that recognise pathogen patterns are PRRs.","Cytokines and chemokines are small and highly mobile."],"k":"Chemokines = chemo-attracting cytokines.","rn":[["","What are chemokines?","What are chemokines?","Subset of cytokines; Attract cells to the site of infection"]]},{"id":36063,"t":"Soluble Factors","s":"The immune system is said to rely on two components. Which pair is correct?","o":["Cells (leucocytes) and soluble factors","Red bone marrow and the spleen only","Innate barriers together with antibiotics given during infection","Lymph nodes and the thymus alone","PAMPs and DAMPs"],"e":"Immune function relies on two components: the cells (leucocytes) and the soluble factors, which are molecules produced by immune cells (cytokines) or associated with immune function (complement). Lysozyme is another soluble factor listed.","w":["These are tissues, not the two components of immune function.","Antibiotics are drugs, not part of the immune system.","These are lymphoid tissues, not the two components described.","PAMPs and DAMPs are patterns recognised by receptors, not components of the immune system."],"k":"Immune function = leucocytes + soluble factors.","rn":[["","What 2 components does immune system function rely on?","What 2 components does immune system function rely on?","Cells (leukocytes); Soluble factors"]]},{"id":36064,"t":"Soluble Factors","a":["complement","complement system","the complement cascade","complement cascade"],"s":"Name the family of plasma proteins that activate one another in a cascade and can destroy bacteria by forming the membrane attack complex.","e":"Complement is a family of plasma proteins that activate each other in the complement cascade. They destabilise bacterial membranes, coat bacteria for destruction and attract phagocytes. The cascade forms the membrane attack complex (MAC), which punches holes in pathogen membranes, causing lysis.","k":"Complement: plasma protein cascade forming the MAC.","rn":[["","What does the complement cascade form?","What does the complement cascade form?","Membrane Attack Complex (MAC)"],["","What is complement?","What is complement?","Family of plasma proteins that activate each other; The \"complement cascade\""]]},{"id":36065,"t":"Soluble Factors","s":"Complement coats a bacterium, making it more likely to be eaten by phagocytes. How does the lecturer describe the effect of this coating?","o":["It makes the pathogen appear more tasty to phagocytes","It hides the pathogen from the phagocytes completely","It converts the bacterium into a self antigen that is tolerated","It stops the phagocyte from forming a phagosome around it","It prevents antibodies from ever binding the bacterium"],"e":"Complement coats pathogens, making them appear more tasty, in other words more likely to be destroyed by phagocytosis. It marks them for destruction by antibodies or phagocytosis. The specific term for this coating is garbled in the recording, so only the effect is tested here.","w":["Coating marks the pathogen for destruction rather than hiding it.","Tolerance applies to self and harmless antigens; coating does not create tolerance.","Coating promotes, rather than blocks, phagocytosis.","Complement marks bacteria for destruction by antibodies as well."],"k":"Complement coating makes pathogens more likely to be phagocytosed.","rn":[["","How does complement coating help phagocytes?","How does complement coating help phagocytes?","Makes pathogens easier to phagocytose; Marks them for destruction by antibodies"]]},{"id":36066,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","s":"A T cell activates a neighbouring cell only when their membranes are touching and the signalling molecule stays anchored on the sender's surface. Which mode of action is this?","o":["Juxtacrine","Paracrine","Autocrine","Endocrine","Exocrine, in which the signal is released into a duct"],"e":"Juxtacrine action is like paracrine action but requires direct cell-to-cell contact. Sometimes the ligand is anchored on a cell surface, so binding needs close physical proximity. Paracrine acts on nearby cells, autocrine on the secreting cell itself, and endocrine on distant cells via the circulation.","w":["Paracrine signalling reaches nearby cells without needing direct contact.","Autocrine signalling acts back on the secreting cell itself.","Endocrine signalling travels in the circulation to distant cells.","Exocrine is not one of the immune signalling modes described."],"k":"Juxtacrine = paracrine-like but needing direct cell-to-cell contact.","rn":[["","What is juxtacrine action?","What is juxtacrine action?","Like paracrine but needs direct cell-to-cell contact"]]},{"id":36067,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","s":"Which mode of action accounts for the majority of immune signalling according to the speaker notes?","o":["Paracrine","Endocrine","Autocrine","Juxtacrine","Exocrine"],"e":"The speaker notes state that paracrine action, where messengers reach nearby cells, accounts for the majority of immune signalling. Endocrine action is much less common than for hormones such as insulin, and excessive endocrine activity can be catastrophic, as in anaphylaxis.","w":["Endocrine immune signalling is much less common than for hormones.","Autocrine acts on the cell itself and is not described as the majority.","Juxtacrine needs direct contact and is not described as the majority.","Exocrine is not one of the modes described for immune signalling."],"k":"Paracrine action is the majority of immune signalling.","rn":[["","Which mode is the majority of immune signalling?","Which mode is the majority of immune signalling?","Paracrine"]]},{"id":36068,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","s":"A patient stung by a wasp collapses with widespread effects throughout the body. Over-secretion of immune signalling molecules acting far from where they were released is suspected. Which mode of action is responsible, and what is the condition?","o":["Endocrine action; anaphylaxis","Juxtacrine action; autoimmunity","Autocrine action; graft rejection","Paracrine action; immunodeficiency","Endocrine action; tolerance"],"e":"Endocrine action carries messengers through the circulation to distant cells. It is much less common in immune signalling than for hormones, but inappropriate and excessive endocrine activity from over-secretion of immune signalling molecules can be catastrophic, for example in anaphylaxis. Anaphylactic shock is the extreme form of allergy.","w":["Juxtacrine needs direct contact and does not explain whole-body effects; autoimmunity is attack on self.","Autocrine acts on the secreting cell; graft rejection follows transplantation.","Paracrine acts locally; immunodeficiency is failed immunity.","Tolerance is non-reactivity, the opposite of this reaction."],"k":"Excessive endocrine immune signalling can be catastrophic, as in anaphylaxis.","rn":[["","What can excessive immune endocrine signalling cause?","What can excessive immune endocrine signalling cause?","Anaphylaxis (catastrophic)"]]},{"id":36069,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","s":"A macrophage releases a cytokine that binds receptors on its own surface and alters its own activity. Which statement fits this signalling?","o":["It is autocrine and may stimulate or inhibit the cell as the response needs","It is endocrine and must travel through the bloodstream first","It is juxtacrine and requires contact with another cell","It is paracrine and acts only on cells far away","It is impossible, because cells never respond to messengers that they themselves secrete"],"e":"In autocrine action the secreting cell's own messengers bind back to itself. The speaker notes say this may stimulate or inhibit cellular activity as needed in the ongoing response. Paracrine acts on nearby cells, juxtacrine on adjacent cells in contact, and endocrine on distant cells via the circulation.","w":["Endocrine signalling reaches distant cells via the circulation.","Juxtacrine signalling needs contact with an adjacent cell.","Paracrine signalling acts on nearby, not far, cells.","Autocrine signalling is exactly a cell responding to its own messengers."],"k":"Autocrine: the cell's own messenger acts back on itself to stimulate or inhibit.","rn":[["","What can autocrine cytokine action do?","What can autocrine cytokine action do?","Stimulate or inhibit the cell; As needed in the ongoing response"]]},{"id":36070,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","s":"Which cells secrete cytokines and chemokines according to the speaker notes?","o":["A wide variety of leucocytes, including lymphocytes, macrophages and granulocytes","Only B cells, as part of antibody production","Only the stromal cells of the red bone marrow, which support haematopoiesis in infection","Only the epithelial cells lining the airways","Only cells of the thymus during T cell maturation"],"e":"Cytokines and chemokines are typically low molecular weight proteins, synthesised and secreted by a wide variety of leucocytes, including lymphocytes, macrophages, neutrophils, basophils and eosinophils, amongst others. All members of the leucocyte family communicate using them.","w":["Many leucocytes, not only B cells, secrete cytokines.","The notes list leucocytes as the source, not marrow stromal cells.","The notes list leucocytes, not airway epithelium.","Many leucocytes secrete them, not only cells in the thymus."],"k":"Cytokines/chemokines are secreted by many leucocytes.","rn":[["","Which cells make cytokines and chemokines?","Which cells make cytokines and chemokines?","Lymphocytes; Macrophages, neutrophils; Basophils, eosinophils"]]},{"id":36071,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","s":"During a severe infection, cytokines act on the bone marrow so more white blood cells are produced. Which general principle does this illustrate?","o":["Cytokine binding changes gene expression and therefore cell function","Cytokines act only by punching holes in bacterial membranes","Cytokines work only in the circulation and never on tissues","Cytokines block all differentiation of white blood cells in the bone marrow during infection","Cytokines are PAMPs found on lower organisms"],"e":"Cytokines and chemokines bind to receptors on target cells, triggering signal transduction cascades that lead to changes in gene expression and therefore cell function. Examples include migration to a site of infection and enhanced differentiation in the bone marrow, so more white cells are produced when needed.","w":["Punching holes is the action of the complement MAC.","Most immune signalling is paracrine, acting locally on tissues.","Cytokines enhance differentiation in infection rather than block it.","PAMPs are pathogen structures, whereas cytokines are host proteins."],"k":"Cytokine binding → signalling cascade → changed gene expression and cell function.","rn":[["","How do cytokines change cell function?","How do cytokines change cell function?","Bind target receptors; Signalling cascade; Changes in gene expression"]]},{"id":36072,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","a":["paracrine","paracrine action","paracrine signalling"],"s":"Name the mode of action in which a secreted messenger acts on nearby cells.","e":"Paracrine action is when messengers reach a nearby cell. It accounts for the majority of immune signalling. Juxtacrine is similar but needs direct cell-to-cell contact.","k":"Paracrine = nearby cells.","rn":[["","What is paracrine action?","What is paracrine action?","Cytokine acts on nearby cells"],["","What is juxtacrine action?","What is juxtacrine action?","Like paracrine but needs direct cell-to-cell contact"],["","What are the 4 modes of leucocyte communication?","What are the 4 modes of leucocyte communication?","Autocrine; Paracrine; Endocrine; Juxtacrine"]]},{"id":36073,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts the four modes of action?","o":["Juxtacrine needs contact; endocrine travels through the circulation to distant cells","Autocrine needs contact; paracrine travels through the circulation","Endocrine acts on the secreting cell; autocrine on distant cells","Paracrine needs contact; juxtacrine acts on distant cells","All four modes require direct cell-to-cell contact between the sending and receiving cells"],"e":"Autocrine acts on the secreting cell itself; paracrine on nearby cells; juxtacrine on an adjacent cell in direct contact; endocrine on distant cells via the circulation. Juxtacrine is like paracrine but needs cell-to-cell contact.","w":["Autocrine acts on itself, and paracrine acts locally rather than via the circulation.","Endocrine is distant and autocrine acts on itself.","Paracrine does not need contact, and juxtacrine acts on an adjacent cell.","Only juxtacrine needs direct contact."],"k":"Auto = self; para = nearby; juxta = touching; endo = distant via blood.","rn":[["","What is juxtacrine action?","What is juxtacrine action?","Like paracrine but needs direct cell-to-cell contact"],["","What is endocrine action (immune)?","What is endocrine action (immune)?","Cytokine enters circulation; Acts on distant cells"]]},{"id":36074,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"A patient with severe combined immune deficiency (SCID) has very few functional lymphocytes. Which defence would remain largely intact?","o":["Lysozyme in tears and the acid barrier of the stomach","Antibody production by B cells against the pathogens that infect the patient","Killing of virus-infected cells by T cells","Adaptive memory against previous infections","T cell orchestration of immune activation"],"e":"SCID is an inherited failure of immunity affecting lymphocytes, the cells of adaptive immunity. Barriers such as lysozyme in tears, nasal secretions and saliva and the low pH of the stomach are innate defences that do not depend on lymphocytes. Antibody production, T cell killing, coordination and adaptive memory all depend on lymphocytes.","w":["Antibody production depends on B lymphocytes.","Killing of virus-infected cells depends on T lymphocytes.","Memory is a feature of adaptive (lymphocyte) immunity.","Orchestration is a T cell function."],"k":"Innate barriers do not depend on lymphocytes.","rn":[["","What are the 5 barrier examples in innate immunity?","What are the 5 barrier examples in innate immunity?","Skin; Lysozyme; Mucus and cilia; Low stomach pH and digestive enzymes; Intestinal flora"],["","Where is lysozyme found?","Where is lysozyme found?","Tears; Nasal secretions; Saliva"]]},{"id":36075,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"A bacterium crosses the skin through a cut. Put the defences in the order they would typically act: (1) adaptive response with memory; (2) skin barrier; (3) innate cells recognising PAMPs.","o":["2 → 3 → 1","3 → 2 → 1","1 → 3 → 2","2 → 1 → 3","3 → 1 → 2"],"e":"Barriers such as skin are the first level of the innate response, stopping pathogens entering. Once a pathogen is inside, innate cells such as macrophages use PRRs to recognise PAMPs and respond within hours. If the innate response fails, the slower adaptive response develops, eliminates the pathogen and leaves memory.","w":["The barrier is breached before innate cells can recognise PAMPs inside the body.","Adaptive immunity takes time and comes after innate defences.","The adaptive response comes last, after innate cells.","Innate cells act after the barrier and before adaptive immunity."],"k":"Barrier → innate cells (PRR/PAMP) → adaptive response with memory.","rn":[["","Barrier systems are part of which immune response?","Barrier systems are part of which immune response?","Innate immune response"],["","What are the 2 ways the immune system can respond?","What are the 2 ways the immune system can respond?","Innate immune response; Adaptive immune response"],["","What are the 2 cells of the adaptive immune response?","What are the 2 cells of the adaptive immune response?","B cells; T cells"]]},{"id":36076,"t":"Leucocyte Lineages","s":"Which pairing of immune feature and lineage is NOT correct?","o":["NK cells: myeloid lineage","Neutrophils: myeloid lineage","B cells: lymphoid lineage","Mast cells: myeloid lineage","Cytotoxic T cells: lymphoid lineage"],"e":"The speaker notes list basophils, eosinophils, neutrophils, monocytes, macrophages, dendritic cells, platelets and erythrocytes as myeloid, with mast cells on the myeloid side of the figure. The lymphoid lineage gives cytotoxic T cells, T helper cells, B cells, dendritic cells and natural killer (NK) cells. NK cells are therefore lymphoid.","w":["Neutrophils are myeloid.","B cells are lymphoid.","Mast cells are shown on the myeloid side.","Cytotoxic T cells are lymphoid."],"k":"NK cells are lymphoid, not myeloid.","rn":[["","What non-leucocyte cells also come from the myeloid lineage?","What non-leucocyte cells also come from the myeloid lineage?","Dendritic cells; Platelets; Erythrocytes"],["","What cells come from the lymphoid lineage?","What cells come from the lymphoid lineage?","Cytotoxic T cells; T helper cells; B cells; Dendritic cells; NK cells"],["","Which lineages give rise to dendritic cells?","Which lineages give rise to dendritic cells?","Both myeloid and lymphoid"]]},{"id":36077,"t":"Leucocyte Lineages","s":"Most myeloid cells are responsible for which type of immune response?","o":["The innate immune response","The adaptive immune response","The tolerance of self antigens","The production of antibodies","The maturation of T cells in the thymus"],"e":"Myeloid cells continue to mature in the bone marrow before entering the blood as white blood cells, and most myeloid cells are responsible for the innate immune response. Lymphoid cells begin maturing in the marrow and then go on to other organs such as the thymus, or enter the circulation as B cells do.","w":["The adaptive response is driven largely by lymphoid B and T cells.","Tolerance is a state of non-reactivity, not a lineage function.","Antibodies are made by lymphoid B cells.","T cell maturation concerns lymphoid cells."],"k":"Most myeloid cells mediate innate immunity.","rn":[["","Most myeloid cells are responsible for which response?","Most myeloid cells are responsible for which response?","Innate immune response"]]},{"id":36078,"t":"Innate and Adaptive Response","s":"A researcher notes that the innate response \"sees every pathogen as new\". Which practical consequence follows?","o":["A second infection with the same pathogen is met with no faster innate response","The innate response gets faster with each repeat exposure","The innate response produces long-lived memory cells","The innate response adapts to recognise a vast variety of antigens","The innate response cannot start until antibodies against the pathogen have been made by B cells"],"e":"Innate immunity is not adaptable: it treats every pathogen as new, so innate cells are not trained to respond faster on a second meeting. It is rapid, starting within hours, but has no memory. Memory and recognition of a vast variety of antigens are features of adaptive immunity.","w":["Innate cells do not speed up on repeat exposure.","Memory cells belong to adaptive immunity.","Recognition of a vast variety of antigens is adaptive.","Innate immunity starts within hours without antibodies."],"k":"Innate immunity has no memory: no faster response on re-exposure.","rn":[["","What happens if the innate immune response fails?","What happens if the innate immune response fails?","Adaptive response initiates; Elimination of pathogen and recovery"],["","What is the sequence from infection to protection on re-infection?","What is the sequence from infection to protection on re-infection?","Innate immunity (may fail: disease); Adaptive immunity: recovery; Specific immunologic memory: no disease"],["","Why does innate immunity sometimes fail?","Why does innate immunity sometimes fail?","It cannot adapt; Sees every pathogen as new"]]},{"id":36079,"t":"What Is Immunity","s":"Which pairing of disorder and type of immune imbalance is correct?","o":["Anaphylaxis: overactive allergy","AIDS: overactive autoimmunity","Graft rejection: underactive immunity","SCID: overactive allergy","Autoimmune disease: excess tolerance"],"e":"Anaphylactic shock is the extreme form of allergy, an overactive state. AIDS and SCID are immunodeficiencies (underactive). Graft rejection and autoimmunity are overactive states; autoimmune disease results from a breakdown of tolerance, not excess tolerance.","w":["AIDS is an acquired immunodeficiency, an underactive state.","Graft rejection is an overactive state.","SCID is an inherited immunodeficiency.","Autoimmune disease follows a breakdown of tolerance."],"k":"Anaphylaxis = extreme allergy (overactive).","rn":[["","What are the 3 results of an overactive immune system?","What are the 3 results of an overactive immune system?","Allergies; Graft rejection; Autoimmunity"]]},{"id":36080,"t":"Leucocyte Lineages","s":"Why is bone marrow function described as really important for the health of the immune system?","o":["All blood cells, including leucocytes, start from stem cells there","It is the only place where antigens are presented to lymphocytes","It is where lymphocytes cross from the blood into the lymph through high endothelial venules","It stores blood for emergencies and cleanses it of pathogens","It is the site where T cells complete their maturation"],"e":"All blood cells start from one cell, the haematopoietic stem cell in the red bone marrow, which differentiates into the myeloid and lymphoid lineages. Myeloid cells mature there before entering the blood, and lymphoid cells begin maturing there. So bone marrow function is central to producing all immune cells.","w":["Antigen presentation happens in many tissues, including lymph nodes and skin.","HEVs are in lymph nodes.","The emergency blood store is the spleen.","T cells complete maturation in the thymus."],"k":"All leucocytes originate from bone marrow stem cells.","rn":[["","Which cell do all blood cells start from?","Which cell do all blood cells start from?","Haematopoietic stem cell"]]},{"id":36081,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","s":"A patient has a defect in the enzymes linked to the intracellular domains of phagocyte receptors, so they cannot phosphorylate one another. Which step would fail?","o":["Triggering of phagocytosis after PAMP cross-linking","Production of PAMPs by bacteria","Release of bradykinin from damaged tissue","Movement of lymphocytes through HEVs","Secretion of lysozyme into the tears and nasal secretions of the patient"],"e":"PAMP-mediated cross-linking drags receptor complexes together so their associated enzymes phosphorylate each other. This phosphorylation triggers a signal transduction pathway that leads to phagocytosis. Without mutual phosphorylation, binding alone would not trigger engulfment.","w":["PAMPs are made by the bacteria, not by host receptor enzymes.","Bradykinin release follows tissue rupture and is unrelated.","HEV crossing depends on lymphocyte adhesion, not phagocyte receptor enzymes.","Lysozyme secretion is unrelated to receptor phosphorylation."],"k":"Receptor phosphorylation after cross-linking is needed to trigger phagocytosis.","rn":[["","Phosphorylation after cross-linking triggers what?","Phosphorylation after cross-linking triggers what?","Signal transduction pathway; Leading to phagocytosis"]]},{"id":36082,"t":"Pattern Recognition Receptors","s":"An immune cell binds a PAMP. Besides destroying the pathogen, what else does the lecture say this binding starts?","o":["Signalling communication within the immune system","Tolerance towards the pathogen","Production of new PAMPs by the macrophage","Release of red blood cells from the spleen","Shutdown of all inflammatory responses"],"e":"A PAMP recognised by a PRR on a leucocyte such as a macrophage leads to events that first destroy the pathogen and also start signalling communication within the immune system. Phagocytosis often comes with release of soluble molecules that activate other parts of the immune system.","w":["Recognition of a PAMP leads to attack, not tolerance.","PAMPs are pathogen structures, not made by host cells.","The spleen's blood store is unrelated to PAMP recognition.","PAMP recognition promotes, rather than shuts down, the immune response."],"k":"PAMP recognition destroys the pathogen and starts immune signalling.","rn":[["","What does PRR (pattern recognition receptor) binding to a PAMP (pathogen associated mol…","What does PRR (pattern recognition receptor) binding to a PAMP (pathogen associated molecular pattern) lead to?","Destruction of the pathogen; Signalling to other immune cells"],["","What often follows initiation of phagocytosis?","What often follows initiation of phagocytosis?","Release of soluble molecules; They activate other parts of the immune system"]]},{"id":36083,"t":"Pattern Recognition Receptors","s":"Which statement about leucocyte receptors is correct?","o":["Some bind host molecules made during the response, amplifying it","They recognise only molecules from pathogens and nothing else","They are found only on the outer cell membrane, never inside the cell","They work alone and never use adapter molecules","They bind ligands only with very low affinity"],"e":"Leucocytes have receptors for molecules that are not from pathogens but are produced as part of the immune response, and these amplify the response. PRRs are found on the membrane and in endosomes. Receptors often rely on adapter molecules, and binding is specific with moderate to high affinity.","w":["Leucocytes also have receptors for host molecules such as cytokines.","Intracellular PRRs exist in endosomes.","Leucocyte receptors often rely on adapter molecules.","Binding is typically of moderate to high affinity."],"k":"Leucocytes also respond to host molecules produced in the response, amplifying it.","rn":[["","What other receptors do leucocytes carry besides pathogen-detecting ones?","What other receptors do leucocytes carry besides pathogen-detecting ones?","Receptors for molecules made in the immune response; They amplify the immune response"]]},{"id":36084,"t":"Immune System Tissues","s":"Which combination correctly lists the immune tissues by lineage?","o":["Red bone marrow: myeloid; lymph nodes, spleen and thymus: lymphoid","Red bone marrow and spleen: myeloid; lymph nodes and thymus: lymphoid","All four tissues are part of the myeloid lineage","Thymus: myeloid; bone marrow, spleen and lymph nodes: lymphoid","Lymph nodes: myeloid; marrow, spleen and thymus: lymphoid"],"e":"The tissues table assigns red bone marrow to the myeloid lineage (site of production of all blood cells) and lymph nodes, spleen and thymus to the lymphoid lineage. The thymus matures T cells, the spleen is an emergency blood store, and lymph nodes host resident lymphocytes and macrophages.","w":["The spleen is listed as lymphoid.","Lymph nodes, spleen and thymus are lymphoid.","The thymus is lymphoid.","Lymph nodes are lymphoid."],"k":"Marrow = myeloid; lymph nodes, spleen, thymus = lymphoid.","rn":[["","What are the 4 immune system tissues?","What are the 4 immune system tissues?","Red bone marrow; Lymph nodes; Spleen; Thymus"]]},{"id":36085,"t":"Immune System Tissues","s":"A patient's spleen is removed after trauma. Based on the roles given in the lecture, which function would be lost?","o":["An emergency blood store that also cleanses blood of pathogens and debris","The production of all blood cells, including B and T lymphocytes","The site of maturation of T lymphocytes","The crossing of lymphocytes from blood to lymph through HEVs","The release of lysozyme into saliva"],"e":"The spleen acts as an emergency blood store, and blood passing through it is cleansed of pathogens and debris. Red bone marrow produces all blood cells, the thymus matures T cells, and HEVs are in lymph nodes.","w":["All blood cells are produced in red bone marrow.","T cells mature in the thymus.","HEVs are in lymph nodes.","Lysozyme is in tears, nasal secretions and saliva."],"k":"Spleen: emergency blood store; cleanses blood of pathogens and debris.","rn":[["","What is the main job of the spleen?","What is the main job of the spleen?","\"Emergency\" blood store; Cleanses blood of pathogens and debris"]]},{"id":36086,"t":"What Is Immunity","a":["immunodeficiency","immune deficiency","immunodeficient"],"s":"What term describes the outcome of failed immunity, whether inherited or acquired?","e":"Immunodeficiency is the outcome of failed immunity. It may be inherited, as in SCID, or acquired during life, as in AIDS. Patients may die from opportunistic infections.","k":"Immunodeficiency = failed immunity (inherited or acquired).","rn":[["","What is Immunodeficiency?","What is Immunodeficiency?","Outcome of failed immunity"]]},{"id":36087,"t":"Immune Tolerance","a":["tolerance","immune tolerance","immunological tolerance"],"s":"What is the term for a state of immunological non-reactivity to an antigen?","e":"Tolerance is a state of immunological non-reactivity to an antigen. It protects self antigens, harmless environmental antigens and commensal organisms; its breakdown leads to autoimmune disease.","k":"Tolerance = non-reactivity to an antigen.","rn":[["","What is Tolerance?","What is Tolerance?","State of immunological non-reactivity to an antigen"]]},{"id":36088,"t":"Immune System Tissues","s":"An infant is born without a thymus. Which cells would be most directly affected?","o":["T lymphocytes, which cannot complete maturation","Neutrophils, which coordinate acute inflammation","Erythrocytes, which come from the erythroid progenitor","Mast cells, which facilitate the acute inflammatory response","Eosinophils, which defend against nematodes"],"e":"The thymus is the site of maturation of T lymphocytes. T-cell progenitors emerge from the lymphoid progenitor in the bone marrow and migrate to the thymus, where they become T helper or cytotoxic cells. Neutrophils, erythrocytes, mast cells and eosinophils are myeloid cells that do not depend on the thymus.","w":["Neutrophils are myeloid and mature in the bone marrow.","Erythrocytes are myeloid.","Mast cells are myeloid.","Eosinophils are myeloid."],"k":"No thymus → no T cell maturation.","rn":[["","What is the main job of the thymus?","What is the main job of the thymus?","Maturation of T lymphocytes"]]},{"id":36089,"t":"Innate and Adaptive Response","s":"Which statement about immunity in the course is consistent with the lecture?","o":["Innate immunity is a useful first line; the most robust response needs antibodies","Innate immunity alone always clears infections without adaptive help","Antibodies bind non-specifically to any foreign molecule","The adaptive response is the first line of defence","Tolerance means attacking harmless antigens"],"e":"The take-home message is that innate immunity is a useful first line of defence, but the most robust response needs the adaptive immune response, where antibodies bind specifically to epitopes on non-self antigens and use a variety of mechanisms to inhibit or destroy the pathogen.","w":["The innate response can fail because it cannot adapt.","Antibodies bind specifically to epitopes on non-self antigens.","The innate response is the first line of defence.","Tolerance is non-reactivity to harmless antigens."],"k":"Innate = first line; robust response = specific antibodies.","rn":[["","For the most robust response and outcomes, what is needed?","For the most robust response and outcomes, what is needed?","Antibodies"],["","Which immune response is the first line of defence?","Which immune response is the first line of defence?","Innate immunity"]]},{"id":36090,"t":"Cells of the Immune Response","s":"Which of these innate cells is described as highly migratory, moving to tissues when needed, and keeping antigen to show other immune cells after digesting a pathogen?","o":["Macrophage","Eosinophil that targets nematode parasites","Basophil that targets multicellular parasites","Mast cell that facilitates acute inflammation","Natural killer cell from the lymphoid line"],"e":"Macrophages destroy pathogens or infected cells, are migratory and move to tissues when needed. After digesting a pathogen they keep components (antigens) and present them to other immune cells. They are phagocytes and antigen presenting cells.","w":["Eosinophils defend against nematodes and are not described as antigen presenters.","Basophils defend against multicellular parasites.","Mast cells facilitate acute inflammation.","NK cells are lymphoid and not described in this role."],"k":"Macrophage: migratory phagocyte that presents antigen.","rn":[["","Which 2 innate cells are phagocytes?","Which 2 innate cells are phagocytes?","Macrophage; Neutrophil"],["","What are the 6 cells of the innate immune response?","What are the 6 cells of the innate immune response?","Macrophage; Neutrophil; Eosinophil; Basophil; Mast cell; Dendritic cell"]]},{"id":36091,"t":"Innate and Adaptive Response","s":"In the figure, the arrow from innate immunity to disease is crossed out in one panel and present in another. What does the uncrossed path to disease represent?","o":["Innate immunity acting alone can fail, leading to disease","Adaptive memory always causes disease on re-infection","Tolerance of self antigens breaking down and so causing autoimmune disease","Barriers stopping all pathogens at the skin","Adaptive immunity acting before innate immunity"],"e":"The figure shows that the first infection meets innate immunity first. If innate immunity is the only response it can fail and lead to disease. Adaptive immunity then develops and leads to recovery, leaving specific immunological memory, so that on re-infection memory gives a fast response and no disease.","w":["Memory prevents disease on re-infection; it does not cause it.","The figure is about innate, adaptive and memory responses, not autoimmunity.","The figure shows that pathogens can get past the first line and cause disease.","Innate immunity acts first; adaptive immunity develops later."],"k":"Innate immunity alone can fail; adaptive immunity leads to recovery and memory.","f":"im08","rn":[["","What is the sequence from infection to protection on re-infection?","What is the sequence from infection to protection on re-infection?","Innate immunity (may fail: disease); Adaptive immunity: recovery; Specific immunologic memory: no disease"]]},{"id":36092,"t":"Innate and Adaptive Response","s":"According to the same figure, what happens on re-infection with the same pathogen after recovery?","o":["Specific immunological memory gives a fast response and no disease","Innate immunity fails again and the patient becomes ill once more","The adaptive response takes even longer to develop than it did the first time","Tolerance develops and the pathogen is ignored completely","Barriers become impermeable so the pathogen cannot enter at all"],"e":"After the first infection, adaptive immunity leads to recovery and leaves specific immunological memory. On re-infection (top right of the figure), memory gives a fast response and no disease. The adaptive response starts earlier than the first time.","w":["Memory prevents a repeat illness.","The adaptive response is faster on re-encounter.","Tolerance applies to self and harmless antigens, not pathogens.","The figure does not describe barriers changing."],"k":"Re-infection → memory → fast response, no disease.","f":"im08","rn":[["","What is the sequence from infection to protection on re-infection?","What is the sequence from infection to protection on re-infection?","Innate immunity (may fail: disease); Adaptive immunity: recovery; Specific immunologic memory: no disease"]]},{"id":36093,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"The barriers figure divides defences into biochemical and chemical/physical groups. Which is placed in the biochemical group?","o":["Spermine in semen","Cilia lining the trachea","Acid in the stomach","Intact skin","Mucus"],"e":"The figure lists biochemical barriers as lysozyme in tears, nasal secretions and saliva; sebaceous gland secretions; commensal organisms in gut and vagina; and spermine in semen. Chemical and physical barriers are mucus, cilia lining the trachea, acid in the stomach and skin.","w":["Cilia lining the trachea are a physical barrier.","Stomach acid is in the chemical and physical group.","Skin is in the chemical and physical group.","Mucus is in the chemical and physical group."],"k":"Biochemical barriers: lysozyme, sebaceous secretions, commensals, spermine.","f":"im10"},{"id":36094,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"A patient's sebaceous glands are destroyed by a skin condition. Which group of barrier from the figure has been affected?","o":["A biochemical barrier","A purely physical barrier such as cilia","An adaptive immune barrier","A lymphoid tissue","A soluble factor made by B cells"],"e":"Sebaceous gland secretions are listed in the barriers figure as a biochemical barrier, alongside lysozyme, commensal organisms and spermine. Physical and chemical barriers are mucus, cilia, stomach acid and skin. Barriers are part of innate, not adaptive, immunity.","w":["Cilia are physical; sebaceous secretions are biochemical.","Barriers belong to innate immunity.","Lymphoid tissues are lymph nodes, spleen and thymus.","B cells make antibodies, not sebaceous secretions."],"k":"Sebaceous secretions = biochemical barrier.","f":"im10","rn":[["","What 2 kinds of barrier are part of innate immunity?","What 2 kinds of barrier are part of innate immunity?","Biochemical; Physical"]]},{"id":36095,"t":"Immune System Tissues","s":"On the lymphoid system figure, which labelled structure is found in the gut wall?","o":["Peyer's patches","Adenoids","Thoracic duct","Thymus","Left subclavian vein"],"e":"The lymphoid system figure labels adenoids, left subclavian vein, right lymphatic duct, thymus, thoracic duct, spleen, Peyer's patches, red bone marrow and tissue lymphatics. Peyer's patches are the lymphoid structures shown in the intestine.","w":["Adenoids are shown in the upper airway.","The thoracic duct is a large lymph vessel in the chest.","The thymus is in the chest.","The left subclavian vein is where lymph returns to blood."],"k":"Peyer's patches are gut-associated lymphoid tissue on the figure.","f":"im11","rn":[["","Which lymphoid tissue is labelled in the gut?","Which lymphoid tissue is labelled in the gut?","Peyer's patches"]]},{"id":36096,"t":"Leucocyte Lineages","s":"In the lineage figure, the T-cell progenitor enters a boxed region before becoming a T helper or cytotoxic cell. What is this region?","o":["Thymus","Spleen","Lymph node","Red bone marrow","Peripheral tissues"],"e":"The lineage figure shows the T-cell progenitor arising from the lymphoid progenitor and entering the thymus (boxed), where it becomes a T<sub>H</sub> helper cell or T<sub>C</sub> cytotoxic cell. The middle blue band is the bone marrow and the pink side bands are peripheral tissues.","w":["The spleen is not the boxed region in the lineage figure.","Lymph nodes are where lymphocytes meet antigen, not where T cells mature.","The bone marrow is the central blue band where the stem cell sits.","Peripheral tissues are the pink side bands."],"k":"T cell progenitors mature into helper or cytotoxic T cells in the thymus.","f":"im12","rn":[["","Which 2 cells does the T-cell progenitor give rise to in the thymus?","Which 2 cells does the T-cell progenitor give rise to in the thymus?","T helper cell (TH); Cytotoxic T cell (Tc)"]]},{"id":36097,"t":"Leucocyte Lineages","a":["haematopoietic stem cell","hematopoietic stem cell","hsc","stem cell","haemopoietic stem cell"],"s":"What is the name of the self-renewing cell in the bone marrow from which all blood cells start?","e":"All blood cells start from the haematopoietic stem cell in the bone marrow. It is non-differentiated and self-renewing, and when triggered by messengers such as cytokines it becomes a myeloid or lymphoid progenitor.","k":"The haematopoietic stem cell gives rise to all blood cells.","f":"im12","rn":[["","Which cell do all blood cells start from?","Which cell do all blood cells start from?","Haematopoietic stem cell"],["","What triggers a haematopoietic stem cell to differentiate?","What triggers a haematopoietic stem cell to differentiate?","Molecular messengers (e.g. cytokines); Bind to receptors on the stem cell"],["","What are the 2 progenitors from the haematopoietic stem cell?","What are the 2 progenitors from the haematopoietic stem cell?","Myeloid progenitor; Lymphoid progenitor"]]},{"id":36098,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","s":"In panel B of the phagocytosis figure, the phagocyte's membrane extends around the microbe. What are these extensions called?","o":["Pseudopodia","Cilia","Phagolysosomes","Granules","Endosomes"],"e":"Phagocytosis begins with attachment of the microbe by pattern recognition receptors (panel A), then pseudopodia form a phagosome (panel B). Granules then fuse and kill the microbe, completing the phagolysosome (panel C), and microbial products are released (panel D).","w":["Cilia line the respiratory tract and expel mucus.","The phagolysosome forms later, after granule fusion.","Granules fuse with the phagosome after it has formed.","Endosomes are intracellular compartments with PRRs."],"k":"Pseudopodia wrap around the microbe to form a phagosome.","f":"im18","rn":[["","What are the 5 steps of phagocytosis?","What are the 5 steps of phagocytosis?","Bacterium attaches to pseudopodia; Ingested, forming phagosome; Phagosome fuses with lysosome; Bacterium killed and digested; Digestion products released"]]},{"id":36099,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","s":"Panel A of the cross-linking figure shows two receptors on a phagocyte bound to one microbial antigen. Why does the lecturer say this two-receptor step exists?","o":["To protect against overreaction or non-specific reaction of the immune system","To let a single receptor phosphorylate itself more quickly once it binds the microbe","To make the microbe tolerated as a commensal","To allow the phagocyte to make antibodies","To move lymphocytes through the HEV"],"e":"PRRs can have two components that need to cross-link before phagocytosis starts, which protects against overreaction or a non-specific reaction of the immune system. Cross-linking brings the receptors' enzymes together so they phosphorylate each other, triggering phagocytosis.","w":["Receptor enzymes phosphorylate others of their kind, not themselves.","Cross-linking leads to destruction, not tolerance.","Phagocytes do not make antibodies; B cells do.","HEV crossing concerns lymphocytes in lymph nodes."],"k":"Cross-linking requirement prevents overreaction.","f":"im19","rn":[["","Why is cross-linking needed before phagocytosis?","Why is cross-linking needed before phagocytosis?","Protects against overreaction / non-specific reaction"]]},{"id":36100,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","s":"The leucocyte signalling figure has a green box partway down the cascade. What event does it show?","o":["Receptor-associated adapter molecules being phosphorylated","The ligand binding to its receptor at the outer surface of the cell membrane","Initiation of gene expression in the nucleus","Release of histamine from the cell","Formation of the membrane attack complex"],"e":"In the signalling figure, the red box shows a ligand binding its receptor at the membrane, the green box shows receptor-associated adapter molecules being phosphorylated, and the arrows run down to the nucleus, ending in initiation of gene expression. The individual molecules are not needed for this session.","w":["Ligand binding is in the red box at the membrane.","Gene expression is the endpoint in the nucleus.","Histamine release is not part of this cascade figure.","The MAC is formed by complement."],"k":"Signalling figure: red = ligand binding; green = adapter phosphorylation; nucleus = gene expression.","f":"im20","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of leucocyte signalling?","What are the 3 steps of leucocyte signalling?","Ligand-receptor interaction; Phosphorylation of adapter molecules; Initiation of gene expression"]]},{"id":36101,"t":"Immune System Tissues","s":"In the recirculation pathway figure, through which structure do lymphocytes in the lymph eventually return to the blood?","o":["The thoracic duct","The high endothelial venule","The afferent artery","The thymus","The red bone marrow"],"e":"The blood lymphocyte pool delivers lymphocytes to the lymph node through the HEV, to the spleen and to the tissues. They return via afferent and efferent lymphatics to the thoracic duct and back into the blood. A lymphocyte may complete this circuit 1 to 2 times a day.","w":["The HEV is where lymphocytes leave the blood, not return to it.","Arteries carry blood into tissues, not lymph back to blood.","The thymus is where T cells mature.","The bone marrow is where blood cells are produced."],"k":"Lymphocytes return to blood via the thoracic duct.","f":"ima15","rn":[["","Which 2 ducts return lymph to the blood?","Which 2 ducts return lymph to the blood?","Right lymphatic duct; Thoracic duct"]]},{"id":36102,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","s":"In the signalling-modes figure, one panel shows messengers travelling in the circulation to a distant cell. What is this mode, and how common is it in immune signalling?","o":["Endocrine; much less common than for hormones","Paracrine; the majority of immune signalling","Autocrine; the main way cytokines act","Juxtacrine; occurs only through the blood","Endocrine; the main mode of cytokine action"],"e":"Endocrine action carries messengers through the circulation to distant cells. In immune signalling it is much less common than for hormones such as insulin, and excessive endocrine activity can be catastrophic, as in anaphylaxis. Paracrine action is the majority of immune signalling.","w":["Paracrine acts on nearby cells, not via the circulation.","Autocrine acts back on the secreting cell.","Juxtacrine needs direct contact, not travel through blood.","Endocrine is much less common in immune signalling; paracrine is the majority."],"k":"Endocrine immune signalling is uncommon; paracrine dominates.","f":"im23","rn":[["","Is immune endocrine signalling common?","Is immune endocrine signalling common?","No, much less common than hormonal"]]},{"id":36103,"t":"Immune System Tissues","s":"A patient on long-term treatment that suppresses the bone marrow develops recurrent bacterial infections, and the neutrophil count is very low. Which line of reasoning is best?","o":["Myeloid production is reduced, so fewer phagocytic granulocytes reach infected tissue","Lymphoid production is reduced, so fewer antibodies are made against the invading bacteria in tissue","The thymus has failed, so neutrophils cannot mature","Barriers have become more permeable because lysozyme is lost","Tolerance has broken down, causing an autoimmune attack on bacteria"],"e":"Neutrophils are myeloid cells made in the bone marrow; they are abundant, migratory phagocytic granulocytes that coordinate the acute inflammatory response, and the marrow makes more of them in infection. Suppressing the marrow reduces this supply, so infections are not contained by neutrophils.","w":["Neutrophils are myeloid, not lymphoid, and antibodies are not their job.","Neutrophils do not mature in the thymus.","Lysozyme comes from tears, nasal secretions and saliva, not the marrow.","Attacking bacteria is normal immunity, not autoimmunity."],"k":"Low neutrophils = reduced myeloid phagocytes for acute inflammation.","rn":[["","Which immune tissue is myeloid?","Which immune tissue is myeloid?","Red bone marrow"]]},{"id":36104,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"A child with an inherited defect cannot produce mucus normally, and pathogens are not trapped in the airways. Which further defence would still clear pathogens trapped in the lower respiratory tract if the infection progressed?","o":["Phagocytes such as macrophages recognising PAMPs","Lysozyme in the stomach","Commensal flora in the vagina","Spermine in semen","The thymus filtering bacteria out of the lungs and the airway lymph"],"e":"Barriers are the first level of innate immunity. If a barrier fails, pathogens that enter meet innate cells such as macrophages, which use PRRs to recognise PAMPs, phagocytose the pathogen and signal to other immune cells. Lysozyme is in tears, nasal secretions and saliva, commensals protect gut and vagina, and spermine is in semen.","w":["Lysozyme is found in tears, nasal secretions and saliva, not the stomach.","Vaginal flora do not protect the airways.","Spermine is a barrier in semen.","The thymus matures T cells and does not clear bacteria from the lungs."],"k":"If barriers fail, innate phagocytes recognising PAMPs act next."},{"id":36105,"t":"What Is Immunity","s":"A transplant surgeon explains why a new kidney may be attacked even though it is healthy tissue. Which explanation fits the lecture?","o":["A fully functional immune system recognises a non-self organ and rejects it","The kidney's commensal flora are tolerated","The recipient has an acquired immunodeficiency that turns the immune system against the donor kidney","The new kidney is treated as a harmless food antigen","The kidney releases bradykinin, causing tolerance"],"e":"Graft rejection happens when a non-self organ is transplanted into a recipient with a fully functional immune system. It is listed among the overactive states, along with allergies and autoimmunity. Immunity defends against foreign cells and proteins.","w":["Commensal flora are not the reason a donor kidney is attacked.","Rejection needs a fully functional, not deficient, immune system.","Food antigens are tolerated; the kidney is attacked as non-self.","Bradykinin causes pain and histamine release, not tolerance."],"k":"Graft rejection: functional immune system attacks a non-self organ.","rn":[["","When does graft rejection happen?","When does graft rejection happen?","Non-self organ; transplanted into recipient; with fully functional immune system"]]},{"id":36106,"t":"Immune System Tissues","s":"During a chest infection a patient's neck lymph nodes enlarge, and lymphocytes there are activated. What is the main reason lymph nodes are such important sites?","o":["Lymphocytes meet antigens there and are trained to recognise them","Red blood cells are produced there during infection","T cells complete their maturation in the lymph nodes before entering the circulation","Complement proteins are manufactured there","They store blood for emergencies"],"e":"Lymph nodes are where lymphocytes come across antigens and get trained to recognise them. Many antigens from an infectious agent end up in the lymph nodes, which is why nodes enlarge in infection. They contain resident lymphocytes and macrophages that neutralise pathogens and clear debris.","w":["Blood cells are produced in the red bone marrow.","T cell maturation occurs in the thymus.","The lecture does not describe lymph nodes as where complement is made.","The spleen is the emergency blood store."],"k":"Lymph nodes are where lymphocytes meet and learn antigens.","rn":[["","Why are lymph nodes important for lymphocytes?","Why are lymph nodes important for lymphocytes?","Where they meet antigens; Trained to recognise them"]]},{"id":36107,"t":"Immune Tolerance","s":"A patient's immune system attacks harmless pollen every spring, causing a runny nose and wheeze. Which statement best describes the failure?","o":["An inappropriate response to a harmless environmental antigen (allergy)","Failed immunity leading to opportunistic infection","Breakdown of tolerance to the patient's own self tissues in the airway","Graft rejection of a transplanted organ","Deficient innate barriers in the nose"],"e":"Pollen is a harmless environmental antigen that should be tolerated. Allergy and hypersensitivity result when the immune system responds inappropriately to antigens: mast cells activated by allergens release histamine, causing mucus secretion and bronchial contraction. This is an overactive state.","w":["Immunodeficiency is failed immunity, the opposite of this response.","Autoimmunity is attack on self tissues, but pollen is environmental.","Graft rejection follows transplantation of a non-self organ.","The problem is an excessive response, not missing barriers."],"k":"Allergy = inappropriate response to a harmless environmental antigen.","rn":[["","Harmless environmental antigens: 2 examples?","Harmless environmental antigens: 2 examples?","Food sources; Pollen"],["","What are the 3 groups of potential antigens that are not harmful?","What are the 3 groups of potential antigens that are not harmful?","Own cells and tissues (self antigens); Harmless environmental antigens; Commensal organisms"]]},{"id":36108,"t":"Inflammatory Response","s":"A patient with a cut has localised redness, heat and swelling. Which statement correctly links the sign to its mechanism?","o":["Heat: local temperature rises as capillaries dilate","Swelling: capillaries become impermeable, trapping fluid in blood","Redness: neutrophils leave the area","Heat: complement cools the tissue","Swelling: lymphocytes block the HEVs"],"e":"In inflammation, capillaries dilate and become permeable and local temperature rises. Permeable capillaries let fluid (exudate) and cells into the tissue, causing swelling. Neutrophils migrate into, not out of, the damaged area.","w":["Swelling comes from capillaries becoming permeable and letting fluid out.","Neutrophils migrate to the damaged area.","Complement is a protein cascade and does not cool tissue.","HEVs are in lymph nodes and are not the cause of swelling at a cut."],"k":"Inflammation: dilated, permeable capillaries → redness, heat, swelling.","rn":[["","What happens to local temperature in inflammation?","What happens to local temperature in inflammation?","It rises"],["","What happens to capillaries in inflammation?","What happens to capillaries in inflammation?","Dilate; Become permeable"]]},{"id":36109,"t":"What Is Immunity","s":"Which patient's problem results from too little immune activity?","o":["A patient with AIDS who dies of an opportunistic infection","A patient who has anaphylactic shock after a bee sting","A patient whose transplanted heart is rejected","A patient whose B and T cells attack the joints after T cell control mechanisms fail","A patient with severe hay fever"],"e":"An underactive immune system leads to immunodeficiency, in which the patient may die from opportunistic infections, as in AIDS and inherited immune deficiencies. Anaphylaxis, graft rejection, autoimmunity and hay fever are all overactive states.","w":["Anaphylaxis is an extreme allergic, overactive state.","Graft rejection is an overactive state.","Attack on self tissues is autoimmunity, an overactive state.","Hay fever is an allergy, an overactive state."],"k":"Underactive immunity = immunodeficiency with opportunistic infections.","rn":[["","What do patients with an underactive immune system die from?","What do patients with an underactive immune system die from?","Opportunistic infections"]]},{"id":36110,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"A patient's tears contain no lysozyme due to a gland disorder. Which kind of organism would this barrier have helped to destroy?","o":["Bacteria, by breaking their cell wall","Viruses, by interfering with their replication","Nematodes, by eosinophil degranulation","Fungi, by stomach acid","Self cells, by inducing tolerance"],"e":"Lysozyme is a hydrolytic enzyme in tears, saliva and nasal secretions that breaks glycosidic bonds and destroys the bacterial cell wall. Interferon interferes with viral replication, eosinophils defend against nematodes, and stomach acid acts in the gut.","w":["Interfering with viral replication is the role of interferon.","Nematode defence involves eosinophils, not lysozyme.","Stomach acid is a separate gut barrier.","Lysozyme destroys bacteria; it does not act on self cells."],"k":"Lysozyme destroys the bacterial cell wall.","rn":[["","How does lysozyme work?","How does lysozyme work?","Breaks down glycosidic bonds; Destroys bacterial cell wall"]]},{"id":36111,"t":"Soluble Factors","a":["interferon","interferons","ifn"],"s":"Name the cytokine glycoprotein involved in the fight against viruses by interfering with their replication.","e":"Interferon is a cytokine: a glycoprotein that interferes with viral replication. Cytokines are small, highly mobile proteins and the main signalling molecules of the immune system; interleukins are another example.","k":"Interferon interferes with viral replication.","rn":[["","What is interferon?","What is interferon?","A glycoprotein cytokine; Interferes with viral replication"]]},{"id":36112,"t":"Soluble Factors","a":["membrane attack complex","mac","the membrane attack complex"],"s":"Name the structure formed at the end of the complement cascade that inserts into pathogen membranes and causes lysis.","e":"The complement cascade leads to formation of the membrane attack complex (MAC), which inserts into the membrane of invading pathogens and punches holes of varying diameter, leading to lysis.","k":"MAC: complement structure that lyses pathogens.","rn":[["","What does the complement cascade form?","What does the complement cascade form?","Membrane Attack Complex (MAC)"],["","What does the Membrane Attack Complex do?","What does the Membrane Attack Complex do?","Punches holes in pathogen membrane; Causes lysis"]]},{"id":36113,"t":"Immune Tolerance","a":["autoimmune disease","autoimmunity","autoimmune"],"s":"A breakdown of tolerance to self tissue results in what kind of disease?","e":"An immune response to self tissue, a breakdown of tolerance, results in autoimmune disease. The speaker notes describe autoimmunity as happening when T cell control mechanisms fail and B and T cells attack self tissues.","k":"Breakdown of self tolerance → autoimmune disease.","rn":[["","What results from an immune response to self tissue?","What results from an immune response to self tissue?","Breakdown of tolerance; Autoimmune disease"]]},{"id":36114,"t":"Cells of the Immune Response","a":["neutrophil","neutrophils"],"s":"Name the abundant, migratory phagocytic granulocyte that coordinates the acute inflammatory response.","e":"Neutrophils are phagocytes and granulocytes that are highly abundant and migratory, and they coordinate the acute inflammatory response. They migrate to damaged areas, engulf and destroy bacteria, and the marrow produces more of them in infection.","k":"Neutrophils coordinate acute inflammation.","rn":[["","What are the key features of neutrophils?","What are the key features of neutrophils?","Phagocyte, granulocyte; Highly abundant and migratory; Coordinates acute inflammatory response"],["","What do neutrophils do in inflammation?","What do neutrophils do in inflammation?","Migrate to damaged area; Engulf and destroy bacteria"]]},{"id":36115,"t":"Lymphocyte Recirculation","s":"Why do lymphocytes have to recirculate so widely rather than wait in one place?","o":["Only about 1 in 100,000 binds a given antigen, so each must search widely","Each lymphocyte binds every antigen, so it must reach all tissues to work","Lymphocytes lose their specificity unless they keep moving through the blood","Lymphocytes must regularly return to the thymus to be retrained","Lymphocytes are made in lymph nodes and must be carried away"],"e":"Approximately 1 in 100,000 lymphocytes will bind to a particular antigen, so each must travel widely to stand a chance of detecting antigen. Recirculation, 1 to 2 circuits a day, maximises the number of committed lymphocytes meeting antigen.","w":["Each lymphocyte binds a particular antigen, not every antigen.","Specificity is not described as being lost without movement.","Recirculation is through blood and lymph, not repeated thymic retraining.","Blood cells are produced in red bone marrow."],"k":"Rare specificity (1 in 100,000) requires wide recirculation.","rn":[["","Why must each lymphocyte travel widely?","Why must each lymphocyte travel widely?","Only 1 in 100,000 binds an antigen"]]},{"id":36116,"t":"Pattern Recognition Receptors","s":"Which statement about PAMPs and DAMPs is consistent with the lecture?","o":["PAMPs come from pathogens; DAMPs are patterns associated with damaged cells","PAMPs and DAMPs are both made by B cells","DAMPs are found only on Gram-positive bacteria","PAMPs are present on all healthy human cells","DAMPs are a type of Toll-like receptor"],"e":"PAMPs are pathogen associated molecular patterns: structures in lower organisms but not in humans, such as lipopolysaccharides and peptidoglycans. Damage associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) are molecular patterns associated with damaged cells, covered in another session.","w":["Neither is produced by B cells.","DAMPs come from damaged cells, not Gram-positive bacteria.","PAMPs are absent from human cells.","DAMPs are patterns, whereas Toll-like receptors are PRRs."],"k":"PAMPs = pathogen patterns; DAMPs = damaged-cell patterns.","rn":[["","What does PRR (pattern recognition receptor) binding to a PAMP (pathogen associated mol…","What does PRR (pattern recognition receptor) binding to a PAMP (pathogen associated molecular pattern) lead to?","Destruction of the pathogen; Signalling to other immune cells"],["","PAMPs","PAMPs","Pathogen Associated Molecular Patterns"],["","What can PAMPs (pathogen associated molecular patterns) be structures of?","What can PAMPs (pathogen associated molecular patterns) be structures of?","Cell wall components; Proteins; Genetic material"]]},{"id":36117,"t":"Soluble Factors","s":"A researcher blocks the release of chemokines at a wound. Which effect is most likely?","o":["Fewer leucocytes are attracted to the site of infection","Lysozyme stops breaking down bacterial cell walls","The MAC can no longer form in bacterial membranes","Bone marrow stem cells lose their ability to self-renew","Lymphocytes can no longer cross HEVs in lymph nodes"],"e":"Chemokines are chemo-attracting cytokines that attract cells to the site of infection. Blocking them would reduce migration of leucocytes such as neutrophils to the wound. Lysozyme, the MAC, stem cell renewal and HEV crossing are separate processes.","w":["Lysozyme acts independently of chemokines.","The MAC is formed by the complement cascade.","Stem cell self-renewal is not chemokine-dependent in the lecture.","HEV crossing depends on endothelial adhesion, not described as chemokine-dependent here."],"k":"Chemokines attract leucocytes to infection sites.","rn":[["","What are chemokines?","What are chemokines?","Subset of cytokines; Attract cells to the site of infection"],["","What 2 effects does cytokine signalling have on leucocytes?","What 2 effects does cytokine signalling have on leucocytes?","Migration to infection site; More leucocyte production in bone marrow"]]},{"id":36118,"t":"Pattern Recognition Receptors","s":"An intracellular pathogen is being broken down inside a macrophage endosome, and pieces of its genetic material are released. Which receptors could detect these pieces?","o":["Endosomal pattern recognition receptors","Antibodies circulating in plasma","HEV endothelial cells","Complement proteins on red blood cells","T cell receptors on the macrophage"],"e":"PRRs are located on the cell membrane and intracellularly in endosomes. Intracellular (endosomal) PRRs can recognise pathogen components such as genetic material in the process of infecting the cell, after entry, or as they are degraded in the cell.","w":["Antibodies in plasma do not detect components inside an endosome.","HEVs are vessels in lymph nodes.","Complement is not described as an endosomal sensor.","T cell receptors are found on T cells, not macrophages."],"k":"Endosomal PRRs detect pathogen components inside cells.","rn":[["","What do PRRs (pattern recognition receptors) in endosomes detect?","What do PRRs (pattern recognition receptors) in endosomes detect?","Pathogen components entering the cell (e.g. genetic material); Components being degraded in the cell"],["","What do PRRs (pattern recognition receptors) on the cell membrane detect?","What do PRRs (pattern recognition receptors) on the cell membrane detect?","Pathogens outside the cell; Pathogens about to infect the cell"]]},{"id":36119,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"Which of the following best explains why the stomach is a hostile environment for most pathogens?","o":["Low pH and digestive enzymes that break down proteins","Cilia that sweep pathogens back into the mouth","High levels of spermine in gastric juice","Lysozyme made by the stomach lining","Impermeable keratinised skin lining the stomach"],"e":"The low stomach pH and digestive enzymes make the environment uninhabitable for pathogens. Digestive enzymes break down proteins, including components of pathogens, and the low pH is not habitable for them.","w":["Cilia line the respiratory tract.","Spermine is found in semen.","Lysozyme is in tears, nasal secretions and saliva.","Skin is the external barrier; the stomach's defence is acid and enzymes."],"k":"Stomach barrier: low pH and digestive enzymes.","rn":[["","What do stomach digestive enzymes do to pathogens?","What do stomach digestive enzymes do to pathogens?","Break down proteins; Break down pathogen components"],["","How does the stomach act as a barrier?","How does the stomach act as a barrier?","Low pH; Digestive enzymes; Uninhabitable for pathogens"]]},{"id":36120,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","s":"A macrophage meets a bacterium in the skin. Which sequence of events is most consistent with the lecture?","o":["PRR binds PAMP → receptors cross-link → phagocytosis → antigen presented to other cells","Antibody binds PAMP → T cell kills the macrophage → lysis","Bradykinin binds PAMP → histamine release → tolerance","PAMP enters the nucleus → B cell maturation → antibody","HEV binds PAMP → lymphocyte recirculation → phagocytosis"],"e":"The macrophage's PRR binds a PAMP; cross-linking brings receptor enzymes together, they phosphorylate each other and a signal transduction pathway triggers phagocytosis. Macrophages digest the pathogen, keep its antigens and present them to other immune cells, while also starting immune signalling.","w":["Antibodies are made by B cells; macrophages use PRRs, and T cells do not kill macrophages here.","Bradykinin is released by damaged tissue and does not bind PAMPs.","PAMPs do not enter the nucleus to cause B cell maturation.","HEVs are lymph node vessels, not PAMP receptors."],"k":"Macrophage: PRR–PAMP → cross-link → phagocytosis → antigen presentation.","rn":[["","What triggers phagocytosis?","What triggers phagocytosis?","Receptor-pattern recognition (PRR binds PAMP)"],["","What does PRR (pattern recognition receptor) binding to a PAMP (pathogen associated mol…","What does PRR (pattern recognition receptor) binding to a PAMP (pathogen associated molecular pattern) lead to?","Destruction of the pathogen; Signalling to other immune cells"]]},{"id":36121,"t":"Innate and Adaptive Response","s":"A 5-month-old infant is admitted with a third severe infection since birth. The parents say infections began in the first weeks of life, and the infant has never been exposed to blood products or infected adults. Investigations show very few functioning lymphocytes. The paediatrician suspects the problem is inherited rather than acquired.<br><br>Which condition best fits, and what is the expected outcome without treatment?","o":["Severe combined immune deficiency; death from opportunistic infections","Acquired immune deficiency syndrome; allergy and anaphylactic shock with each new exposure","Autoimmune disease; immune attack on self tissue","Graft rejection; attack on a non-self organ","Allergy; mast cell histamine release on each exposure"],"e":"Step 1: failed immunity is immunodeficiency, and a genetic cause identified at an early age points to severe combined immune deficiency (SCID) rather than acquired immune deficiency syndrome (AIDS), which is acquired through HIV infection. Step 2: the speaker notes say an underactive immune system (immunodeficiency) means the patient will die from opportunistic infections, as with AIDS and genetically inherited immune deficiencies.","w":["AIDS is acquired (HIV infection), and immunodeficiency causes infection, not allergy.","Autoimmunity is an overactive state with attack on self tissue, not repeated infection.","Graft rejection needs a transplanted non-self organ and a fully functional immune system.","Allergy is overactivity of mast cells to allergens, not failed defence."],"k":"Genetic early-onset immunodeficiency (SCID) → opportunistic infections.","rn":[["","What does SCID stand for?","What does SCID stand for?","Severe Combined Immune Deficiency"]]},{"id":36122,"t":"Innate and Adaptive Response","s":"A 22-year-old develops a throat infection with a pathogen she has never met. On day 1 her innate response begins within hours, but she becomes unwell and the infection spreads. By day 7 she recovers. Two years later she is exposed to the same pathogen again, but this time she develops no illness at all.<br><br>Which explanation best accounts for both episodes?","o":["Innate immunity cannot adapt; adaptive immunity cleared it and left memory","Innate immunity learned the pathogen and responded faster and more strongly the second time","Adaptive immunity acted within hours in the first episode","Her barriers became impermeable to all pathogens after infection","Pattern recognition receptors gained memory of the pathogen"],"e":"Step 1: innate immunity responds rapidly (within hours) but cannot adapt; it treats every pathogen as new, so it can fail, leading to disease. Step 2: adaptive immunity takes time to develop but then eliminates the agent (recovery) and leaves specific immunological memory, so on re-infection it starts earlier, eliminating the pathogen quickly and preventing disease.","w":["Innate immunity has no memory; it sees each pathogen as new.","Adaptive immunity takes time to develop on first exposure.","Barriers do not change after infection in this way.","PRRs have no immunological memory; they are set by our genes."],"k":"Innate: fast, may fail, no memory; adaptive: slow first time, then memory prevents disease.","rn":[["","Innate vs adaptive immunity: ability to adapt?","Innate vs adaptive immunity: ability to adapt?","Innate: cannot adapt, can fail; Adaptive: recognises vast variety of antigens"],["","What is the sequence from infection to protection on re-infection?","What is the sequence from infection to protection on re-infection?","Innate immunity (may fail: disease); Adaptive immunity: recovery; Specific immunologic memory: no disease"],["","Why does innate immunity sometimes fail?","Why does innate immunity sometimes fail?","It cannot adapt; Sees every pathogen as new"]]},{"id":36123,"t":"Phagocytosis and Signalling","s":"A 60-year-old man has a skin wound infected by bacteria. A tissue macrophage meets a bacterium whose outer membrane contains lipopolysaccharide. When only one receptor on the macrophage touches the bacterium, nothing happens. When the bacterium binds two receptor complexes at once, the macrophage begins to engulf it.<br><br>Which mechanism explains why engulfment begins only after two receptors are bound?","o":["Cross-linking brings receptor enzymes together so they phosphorylate each other","One receptor phosphorylates itself, which is too slow and too weak to trigger engulfment","Two receptors are needed to make antibody against the bacterium","Lipopolysaccharide must enter the nucleus to start phagocytosis","The bacterium must be coated with lysozyme before binding"],"e":"Step 1: lipopolysaccharide is a PAMP (pathogen-associated molecular pattern) recognised by pattern recognition receptors (PRRs) on innate cells such as macrophages. Step 2: the receptors' intracellular domains carry enzymes that phosphorylate others of their kind but not themselves; membrane structure normally keeps them apart, so PAMP cross-linking drags them together, phosphorylation occurs and signal transduction leads to phagocytosis. The lecturer described this as protection against overreaction or non-specific reaction.","w":["The enzymes cannot phosphorylate themselves at all.","Macrophages do not make antibody; B cells do.","The signal is transduced from the membrane; LPS does not enter the nucleus.","Lysozyme breaks down bacterial cell walls; it is not a coating for binding."],"k":"PAMP cross-linking → mutual phosphorylation → phagocytosis.","rn":[["","What does PAMP-mediated cross-linking do?","What does PAMP-mediated cross-linking do?","Drags receptor complexes together; Phosphorylation occurs"]]},{"id":36124,"t":"Lymphocyte Recirculation","s":"A research patient receives a drug that blocks lymphocytes from leaving the blood at high endothelial venules (HEVs). His B and T cells remain in the circulation in normal numbers. A week later, a pathogen enters a lymph node draining his foot, where many of its antigens collect.<br><br>What is the most likely consequence for his adaptive response?","o":["Fewer lymphocytes meet the antigen, as only 1 in 100,000 binds it","Lymphocytes would meet antigen faster because they stay in blood","Innate barriers would fail, allowing the pathogen through the skin","The thymus would stop producing all blood cells","Antibodies would become restricted to the lymph nodes only"],"e":"Step 1: HEVs are the sites in lymph nodes where lymphocytes cross from blood to the lymphatic system; their tall endothelial cells help lymphocytes pass through the venule walls. Step 2: only about 1 in 100,000 lymphocytes binds a particular antigen, so each must travel widely (circuiting 1–2 times a day) to meet antigen. Blocking HEV crossing stops lymphocytes reaching the node where antigens collect, so fewer meet the antigen.","w":["Antigens collect in lymph nodes, so staying in blood reduces encounters.","Skin barriers are not affected by HEV function.","Red bone marrow produces all blood cells; the thymus matures T cells.","Antibodies are not restricted to one site; B cells and antibodies access all areas."],"k":"HEV entry lets rare antigen-specific lymphocytes reach lymph nodes.","rn":[["","Why must each lymphocyte travel widely?","Why must each lymphocyte travel widely?","Only 1 in 100,000 binds an antigen"]]},{"id":36125,"t":"Inflammatory Response","s":"A 30-year-old cuts her hand on a rusty gate. Within minutes the area is painful, red, warm and swollen. Hours later, a swab of pus from the wound shows many neutrophils, and later many macrophages.<br><br>Which sequence best explains these findings?","o":["Bradykinin → mast cell histamine → leaky capillaries → neutrophils → macrophages","Histamine → bradykinin → leaky capillaries → neutrophils turn into tissue macrophages","Neutrophils → bradykinin → capillaries constrict → macrophages","Macrophages → B cells → antibodies → neutrophils","Lysozyme → complement → capillaries constrict"],"e":"Step 1: damaged tissues release bradykinin, which causes pain and stimulates mast cells to release histamine; capillaries dilate and become permeable and local temperature rises (redness, warmth, swelling). Step 2: neutrophils migrate to the damaged area to engulf bacteria; monocytes escape the permeable capillaries and mature into macrophages, which engulf invaders and secrete factors that recruit more neutrophils.","w":["Bradykinin comes first and stimulates histamine release; monocytes, not neutrophils, become macrophages.","Capillaries dilate and become permeable, not constrict.","This is an innate inflammatory sequence; antibodies are not the first step.","Capillaries dilate; lysozyme is a barrier enzyme in secretions."],"k":"Inflammation: bradykinin → histamine → permeable capillaries → neutrophils → macrophages.","rn":[["","What does bradykinin stimulate?","What does bradykinin stimulate?","Mast cells to release histamine"]]},{"id":36126,"t":"Leucocyte Communication","s":"A 19-year-old eats a peanut and within minutes develops widespread swelling, wheeze and collapse. Mast cells across his body have released large amounts of immune signalling molecules that reach distant tissues via the blood. In a normal local infection, by contrast, most immune signalling only reaches neighbouring cells.<br><br>Which pair of terms describes the dangerous signalling and the usual signalling?","o":["Endocrine; paracrine","Paracrine; endocrine","Autocrine; juxtacrine","Juxtacrine; autocrine","Endocrine; autocrine"],"e":"Step 1: messengers travelling in the circulation to distant cells act in an endocrine way; inappropriate and excessive endocrine activity from over-secretion of immune signalling molecules can be catastrophic, for example anaphylaxis. Step 2: the majority of immune signalling is paracrine (acting on nearby cells).","w":["This reverses the two modes.","Autocrine acts on the secreting cell itself; juxtacrine needs direct contact.","These are local modes, not via the blood.","Most immune signalling is paracrine, not autocrine."],"k":"Anaphylaxis = harmful endocrine immune signalling; most signalling is paracrine.","rn":[["","What are the 4 modes of leucocyte communication?","What are the 4 modes of leucocyte communication?","Autocrine; Paracrine; Endocrine; Juxtacrine"],["","What can excessive immune endocrine signalling cause?","What can excessive immune endocrine signalling cause?","Anaphylaxis (catastrophic)"]]},{"id":36127,"t":"Barrier Systems","s":"A 70-year-old on long-term acid-suppressing treatment, who also has a dry mouth and dry eyes, develops recurrent infections. His doctor notes that two of his barrier defences are reduced: the low pH environment in his stomach and the antibacterial enzyme in his tears and saliva.<br><br>Which pair of barrier functions has he lost?","o":["An uninhabitable acidic stomach, and lysozyme breaking bacterial cell walls","Mucus trapping pathogens in the airways, and cilia expelling them from the trachea","Impermeable skin, and spermine in semen","Gut flora antagonism, and sebaceous secretions","Complement attack, and interferon release"],"e":"Step 1: low stomach pH and digestive enzymes make the environment uninhabitable for pathogens. Step 2: lysozyme in tears, nasal secretions and saliva breaks glycosidic bonds in the bacterial cell wall, destroying bacteria. Reduced acid and reduced tears/saliva therefore lose both barriers.","w":["Mucus and cilia are respiratory barriers, not stomach acid or tears.","Skin and semen are not described as affected.","Gut flora and sebaceous secretions are different barriers.","Complement and interferon are soluble factors, not these barriers."],"k":"Stomach acid + lysozyme in tears/saliva are innate barriers.","rn":[["","How does lysozyme work?","How does lysozyme work?","Breaks down glycosidic bonds; Destroys bacterial cell wall"]]},{"id":36128,"t":"Leucocyte Lineages","s":"A 45-year-old's bone marrow is damaged so that the myeloid progenitor cannot develop, while the lymphoid progenitor still works. Blood tests over the following weeks are compared with normal.<br><br>Which cells would be expected to fall, and which would be preserved?","o":["Neutrophils and macrophages fall; NK cells and B cells preserved","NK cells and B cells fall; neutrophils, macrophages and eosinophils preserved","T cells fall; macrophages and neutrophils preserved","All leucocytes are preserved because the stem cell survives","Only platelets fall; all white cells are preserved"],"e":"Step 1: the myeloid progenitor gives neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, monocytes/macrophages, mast cells, dendritic cells, platelets and erythrocytes, so these fall. Step 2: the lymphoid progenitor gives NK cells, T-cell progenitors and B cells (and dendritic cells), so these are preserved.","w":["NK and B cells come from the lymphoid side, which still works.","T cells are lymphoid; macrophages and neutrophils are myeloid.","Myeloid cells cannot develop without the myeloid progenitor.","Neutrophils and macrophages are also myeloid and would fall."],"k":"Myeloid: granulocytes, monocytes/macrophages; lymphoid: NK, T, B cells.","rn":[["","What leucocytes come from the myeloid lineage?","What leucocytes come from the myeloid lineage?","Basophils; Eosinophils; Neutrophils; Monocytes and macrophages"],["","Which cells make cytokines and chemokines?","Which cells make cytokines and chemokines?","Lymphocytes; Macrophages, neutrophils; Basophils, eosinophils"],["","Which 4 innate cells are granulocytes?","Which 4 innate cells are granulocytes?","Neutrophil; Eosinophil; Basophil; Mast cell"]]},{"id":36129,"t":"Immune Tolerance","s":"A 28-year-old woman develops an immune attack on her own thyroid tissue. Her immune response to foods, pollen and her gut bacteria remains normal (no reaction). Her doctor explains that T cell control mechanisms have failed for one self antigen.<br><br>Which statement best explains her condition?","o":["Breakdown of tolerance to a self antigen, causing autoimmune disease","Immunodeficiency, with failed defence against infection by her thyroid's own immune cells","Graft rejection, because thyroid tissue is non-self","Allergy, with mast cells releasing histamine to food","Loss of tolerance to commensal gut organisms"],"e":"Step 1: tolerance is a state of immunological non-reactivity to an antigen, covering self antigens, harmless environmental antigens (food, pollen) and commensals. Step 2: when T cell control mechanisms fail and B and T cells attack self tissue, tolerance has broken down, resulting in autoimmune disease. Her tolerance to food, pollen and gut flora is intact.","w":["She is mounting too much response, not too little.","Her thyroid is her own (self) tissue, not a transplant.","She shows no reaction to food.","Her response to gut bacteria remains normal."],"k":"Autoimmunity = breakdown of tolerance to self.","rn":[["","What results from an immune response to self tissue?","What results from an immune response to self tissue?","Breakdown of tolerance; Autoimmune disease"]]},{"id":36130,"t":"Soluble Factors","s":"A 50-year-old has a bacterial infection. Lab studies show that a family of plasma proteins in her blood activates in sequence, coats the bacteria, attracts phagocytes, and forms a structure that inserts into the bacterial membrane. Separately, her infected cells release a glycoprotein that interferes with viral replication.<br><br>Which two soluble factors are described?","o":["Complement (membrane attack complex) and interferon","Lysozyme from tears and saliva, and chemokines that attract phagocytes","Histamine and bradykinin","Antibody and lipopolysaccharide","Peptidoglycan and complement"],"e":"Step 1: complement is a family of plasma proteins that activate each other in a cascade, destabilise bacterial membranes, coat bacteria marking them for destruction and attract phagocytes; the cascade forms the membrane attack complex (MAC), which punches holes leading to lysis. Step 2: interferon is a cytokine, a glycoprotein that interferes with viral replication.","w":["Lysozyme is a hydrolytic enzyme in saliva/tears; chemokines attract cells but do not interfere with viruses.","Histamine and bradykinin are inflammatory mediators, not these factors.","LPS is a bacterial PAMP, not a host factor.","Peptidoglycan is a bacterial cell wall PAMP."],"k":"Complement cascade → MAC; interferon blocks viral replication.","rn":[["","What does the complement cascade form?","What does the complement cascade form?","Membrane Attack Complex (MAC)"]]}]);
